TP.register({id:"lp7-g6",lp:7,grade:"6",mode:"curriculum",checks:6,nextGrade:"7th grade",build(E){const F=E.F;const ico=(n,s=72)=>F.icon(n,s);const STORY_TITLE="Two Hundred and Forty Chairs";const STORY=["The Neighbor Fund supper happens in the school gym on the third Friday in October, and the chairs are Wren's job, because the chairs are nobody's job and Wren is eleven and had not learned yet to say that.","Two hundred and forty chairs. Thirty tables, eight to a table. The stacks live in the cage under the stage and come out six at a time on a flat dolly with one wheel that fights you.","Her uncle Marek called it a small job in August. By the third Friday in October it had grown teeth. Wren worked out that she had ninety minutes and that the dolly held six, which meant forty trips, which meant a trip every two minutes and fifteen seconds, including the stairs.","At six o'clock she had done eleven trips and the gym looked exactly as empty as it had at five.","Mrs. Adeyemi found her at the cage with the dolly tipped on its side. Mrs. Adeyemi is seventy and runs the kitchen, which means the kitchen runs on her the way a clock runs on a spring.","“Who else is doing this,” she said. It was not a question with a question mark in it.","“Nobody,” Wren said.","Mrs. Adeyemi went back through the double doors. Wren heard her say something short. Then the doors opened and kept opening, and the kitchen came out, seven people in aprons who smelled like onions, and the chairs went up the stairs in a line, hand to hand, like a bucket brigade, and the whole thing took nineteen minutes.","Afterward Wren tried to thank them and got nowhere. Mrs. Adeyemi was already back at the pots. “You were on the list for chairs,” she said. “You were not on the list for doing it alone. Those are two different lists.”"];const REPORT_TITLE="The Neighbor Fund";const REPORT_SRC="From the Westmill Register, March 2026. A report by the paper's news desk.";const REPORT=["The Westmill Neighbor Fund is one supper and one checkbook. The supper is held in the school gym on the third Friday in October. Last October it sold 240 tickets at $20 each and raised $4,800, which is the whole of this year's fund.","The checkbook is held by three trustees, and the rules fit on one card. A grant is for a practical thing that a household cannot cover this month and that will not come back next month: a ramp board, a replacement boiler part, a tank of oil, a bus fare, a week of taxi rides to a clinic.","Over the last five years the fund has made 150 grants, totaling $9,000. The largest single grant in the fund's history is $400, for a stairlift repair in 2023.","Last year the fund made 40 grants. 14 of those 40 paid for travel to the Harrow clinic, which is 40 miles from Westmill and is now the nearest place for several kinds of appointment. The trustees report that travel requests have risen every year since the Harrow clinic took over services that used to be at the Westmill surgery.","The fund keeps no staff and no office. The gym is lent and the kitchen is volunteers, so the supper costs the fund nothing and every dollar raised is a dollar granted."];const COL_TITLE="Buy the Van";const COL_SRC="An opinion column by Dominic Rhee, Westmill Register, March 2026. Mr. Rhee has helped at the supper for eleven years.";const COL=["I have carried chairs at eleven Neighbor Fund suppers and I have written out my share of sixty-dollar checks, and I am done with sixty dollars.","Fourteen of last year's forty grants paid for somebody to get to Harrow and back. Fourteen. That is more than a third of everything we did, spent on the same forty-mile problem, one taxi at a time. Next year it will be sixteen, and the year after that it will be eighteen, because the clinic is not moving back.","A seven-year-old minivan with 90,000 miles on it costs $3,900. We have $4,800. Buy the van.","Yes, that leaves $900, and yes, the first year of insurance and upkeep is quoted at $1,450, and no, $900 is not $1,450. I know. We would have to raise the difference or sell more supper tickets, and I would rather spend a year asking this town for $550 than spend another eleven years driving the same road in the back of somebody else's taxi.","A grant is a patch. A van is a road. We have been patching for eleven years and the hole is getting bigger."];const ANNOUNCE_READ=["Announcement from the trustees of the Westmill Neighbor Fund, read at the March town meeting.","The fund holds $4,800. The trustees will decide the question of the van at the April meeting and will not decide it tonight.","There are three things the trustees want the town to know before April. First, the fund has never held money over from one year to the next, and the rules do not allow it. Whatever is not granted by September goes toward the next supper. Second, the van quote of $3,900 is good until the end of May and includes a six-month warranty on the engine. Third, the insurance and upkeep figure of $1,450 is a quote for one year of cover for a vehicle with four named volunteer drivers. Adding a fifth driver raises it.","The trustees are not asking for a vote tonight. They are asking anyone who has used a travel grant in the last two years to write to them before April, because the trustees have the number of those grants and not the shape of them."].join("\n\n");const SRC_A_CARD="Source A. Report: “Rural Clinic Consolidation and Patient Travel: A Five-County Study.” By Dr. Ola Fadel, transport economist, State Rural Health Office, 2025.";const SRC_A_TEXT="Households more than 25 miles from their nearest clinic missed scheduled appointments 2.4 times as often as households within 10 miles. Access to a shared vehicle closed about half of that gap.";const SRC_B_CARD="Source B. A comment posted under a local news story by a user named harrowroad88. No date.";const SRC_B_TEXT="my cousin says those shared vans sit in a lot 6 days a week. waste of money imo. just give people gas cards.";const esc=s=>String(s).replace(/&/g,"&amp;").replace(/</g,"&lt;").replace(/>/g,"&gt;");const para=(ps,st)=>ps.map(p=>`<p${st?` style="${st}"`:""}>${p}</p>`).join("");const pt=t=>`<h3 style="margin:0 0 4px;color:var(--sea);font-size:1.5rem">${t}</h3>`;const srcLine=t=>`<p class="src" style="font-size:.95rem;margin:0 0 .5em">${t}</p>`;const storyBlock=()=>`${pt(STORY_TITLE)}<div class="passage sm">${para(STORY,"margin:0 0 .55em")}</div>`;const reportBlock=()=>`${pt(REPORT_TITLE)}${srcLine(REPORT_SRC)}<div class="passage sm">${para(REPORT,"margin:0 0 .55em")}</div>`;const colBlock=()=>`${pt(COL_TITLE)}${srcLine(COL_SRC)}<div class="passage sm">${para(COL,"margin:0 0 .55em")}</div>`;const lead=t=>`<p class="big" style="margin:0 0 6px">${t}</p>`;const checklist=items=>`<ul class="qlist" style="margin-top:14px">${items.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`;const ul=a=>`<ul class="card-list">${a.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`;const dl=a=>`<dl class="card-list" style="padding-left:0">${a.map(x=>`<dt style="font-weight:700">${x[0]}</dt><dd style="margin:0 0 8px 1em">${x[1]}</dd>`).join("")}</dl>`;function srcCard(head,body){return`<article style="flex:1 1 320px;border:2px solid var(--line);border-radius:16px;padding:12px 16px;background:var(--surface)"><p style="margin:0 0 8px;font-size:1.02rem;font-weight:700">${head}</p><p style="margin:0;font-size:1.2rem;line-height:1.5">${body}</p></article>`}const SOURCES=()=>`<p class="big" style="font-size:1.5rem">Research question: would a shared van help people in Westmill get to appointments at the Harrow clinic?</p><div style="display:flex;gap:16px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:14px">${srcCard(SRC_A_CARD,SRC_A_TEXT)}${srcCard(SRC_B_CARD,SRC_B_TEXT)}</div>`;const bigTable=(head,rows,cap)=>`<div class="tool-scroll"><table class="chart" style="margin:14px auto;font-size:1.26rem">${cap?`<caption style="font-size:1.1rem;padding-bottom:6px">${cap}</caption>`:""}<tr>${head.map(h=>`<th style="text-align:center">${h}</th>`).join("")}</tr>${rows.map(r=>`<tr>${r.map((c,i)=>i?`<td style="text-align:center">${c}</td>`:`<th>${c}</th>`).join("")}</tr>`).join("")}</table></div>`;function plot(o){const m={l:o.yl?76:38,r:26,t:o.title?34:20,b:o.xl?54:30},sx=o.sx,sy=o.sy,W=o.x1*sx,H=o.y1*sy;const X=x=>m.l+x*sx,Y=y=>m.t+(o.y1-y)*sy,gx=o.gx||1,gy=o.gy||1,lx=o.lx||gx,ly=o.ly||gy;let s=`<svg viewBox="0 0 ${W+m.l+m.r} ${H+m.t+m.b}" width="${o.w||W+m.l+m.r}" role="img" aria-label="${o.alt}" style="max-width:100%;height:auto">`;if(o.title)s+=`<text x="${m.l+W/2}" y="21" font-size="17" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)">${o.title}</text>`;s+=`<rect x="${m.l}" y="${m.t}" width="${W}" height="${H}" fill="var(--surface)"/>`;for(let x=0;x<=o.x1+1e-9;x+=gx)s+=`<line x1="${X(x)}" y1="${m.t}" x2="${X(x)}" y2="${m.t+H}" stroke="var(--line)" stroke-width="1"/>`;for(let y=0;y<=o.y1+1e-9;y+=gy)s+=`<line x1="${m.l}" y1="${Y(y)}" x2="${m.l+W}" y2="${Y(y)}" stroke="var(--line)" stroke-width="1"/>`;s+=`<line x1="${m.l}" y1="${Y(0)}" x2="${m.l+W}" y2="${Y(0)}" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="2.5"/><line x1="${X(0)}" y1="${m.t}" x2="${X(0)}" y2="${m.t+H}" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="2.5"/>`;for(let x=0;x<=o.x1+1e-9;x+=lx)s+=`<text x="${X(x)}" y="${Y(0)+18}" font-size="13" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)">${+x.toFixed(3)}</text>`;for(let y=0;y<=o.y1+1e-9;y+=ly)s+=`<text x="${m.l-7}" y="${Y(y)}" font-size="13" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" fill="var(--ink)">${o.ypre||""}${+y.toFixed(3)}</text>`;if(o.xl)s+=`<text x="${m.l+W/2}" y="${m.t+H+46}" font-size="15" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)">${o.xl}</text>`;if(o.yl)s+=`<text transform="translate(20,${m.t+H/2}) rotate(-90)" font-size="15" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)">${o.yl}</text>`;(o.paths||[]).forEach(p=>{s+=`<polyline points="${p.pts.map(q=>X(q[0])+","+Y(q[1])).join(" ")}" fill="none" stroke="${p.c||"var(--sea)"}" stroke-width="3.5" stroke-linejoin="round"/>`});(o.pts||[]).forEach(p=>{s+=`<circle cx="${X(p[0])}" cy="${Y(p[1])}" r="6" fill="var(--coral)" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="1.4"/>`});return s+"</svg>"}const rideGraph=()=>plot({alt:"Graph titled Fuel spent on Harrow round trips. Dollars spent against the number of round trips. A single straight line rises from the point 0 comma 0 through 2 comma 30, 4 comma 60, and 6 comma 90, and goes on to 8 comma 120.",title:"Fuel spent on Harrow round trips",x1:8,y1:120,sx:48,sy:2.3,gx:1,lx:1,gy:15,ly:30,xl:"Round trips, r",yl:"Dollars spent, c",ypre:"$",paths:[{pts:[[0,0],[8,120]]}],pts:[[0,0],[2,30],[4,60],[6,90]]});const rideTable=()=>bigTable(["Round trips, r","0","2","4","6"],[["Dollars of fuel, c","$0","$30","$60","$90"]],"Table R");const milesTable=()=>bigTable(["Miles driven","20","40","60","80"],[["Gallons used","1","2","3","4"]],"The van's fuel use");const grantsGraph=()=>F.barGraph({title:"Travel grants each year",cats:["2021","2022","2023","2024","2025"],vals:[3,6,8,11,14],max:16,step:2});const choiceTable=()=>bigTable(["","Buy the van","Keep making grants"],[["What it costs this year","$3,900, leaving $900","$4,800, about 80 grants at $60 each"],["What it covers","every Harrow round trip, all year","about 80 one-off needs of any kind"],["The known gap","insurance and upkeep quoted at $1,450, so the fund is $550 short","14 of last year's 40 grants paid for the same Harrow trip"],["If nothing changes","travel grants have gone 3, 6, 8, 11, 14 over five years","the fund has never held money over from one year to the next"],["Worth knowing","the quote is good until the end of May and carries a six-month engine warranty","the largest grant the fund has ever made is $400"]],"Two things the $4,800 could buy");const WRITE_DIR="Write on your paper for about 5 minutes. Then hold it up or read it to your teacher.";const POEM_TITLE="Hand to Hand";const POEM=["One pair of hands can lift a chair.","One pair of hands can lift a chair","all evening, and the room still stands","as empty as a drum.","","Many pairs of hands, and the stairs","become a river running up.","Nothing is lighter than it was.","There are only more of us beneath it."];const poemBlock=()=>`${pt(POEM_TITLE)}<p class="src" style="font-size:.95rem;margin:0 0 .5em">A poem by Hanna Oduya, printed beside the supper notice.</p><div class="passage sm" style="line-height:1.7">${POEM.map(l=>l?esc(l):"&nbsp;").join("<br>")}</div>`;function areaFig(){const w=700,h=272,st=`fill="var(--sea-soft)" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="3" stroke-linejoin="round"`;const T=(x,y,t)=>`<text x="${x}" y="${y}" font-size="19" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)">${t}</text>`;let b=`<rect x="3" y="3" width="${w-6}" height="${h-6}" rx="20" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--line)" stroke-width="4"/>`;b+=`<polygon points="60,210 260,210 120,90" ${st}/>`;b+=`<path d="M120 90 V210" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="7 6"/>`;b+=T(160,236,"10 m")+T(146,154,"6 m")+T(160,258,"Plot 1");b+=`<polygon points="380,210 580,210 540,130 420,130" ${st}/>`;b+=`<path d="M420 130 V210 M540 130 V210" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="7 6"/>`;b+=T(480,236,"10 m")+T(480,120,"6 m")+T(600,174,"4 m")+T(480,258,"Plot 2");b+=`<path d="M584 130 H596 M584 210 H596 M590 130 V210" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="2"/>`;return`<svg viewBox="0 0 ${w} ${h}" width="660" role="img" aria-label="Plot 1 is a triangle with a base of 10 metres and a height of 6 metres marked by a dashed line. Plot 2 is a trapezoid with a bottom side of 10 metres, a top side of 6 metres, and a height of 4 metres. Two dashed lines cut the trapezoid into a middle rectangle and a triangle at each end.">${b}</svg>`}const STOPS=[{id:1,title:"Two Hundred and Forty Chairs",short:"The Chairs",parts:[{std:["RL.6.1"],dur:150,dir:"Read the story to yourself. Then answer out loud. Cite the exact words that support each answer.",desc:"citing textual evidence to support what a story says explicitly and inferences drawn from it",html:storyBlock,qs:["How many chairs are there, and how does the story arrive at that number? Cite the words.","Cite the evidence that the job was bigger than Wren had been led to expect.","Cite textual evidence to support this inference: Wren did not ask anybody for help.","Cite the evidence that shows how much faster the work went once the kitchen came out.","At the end Mrs. Adeyemi says, “Those are two different lists.” What does she mean? Cite the words that support your answer."],key:["240. Cites “Two hundred and forty chairs. Thirty tables, eight to a table.” Thirty tables with eight chairs each is 240. (RL.6.1, explicit evidence)","Cites “Her uncle Marek called it a small job in August” against “By the third Friday in October it had grown teeth,” and the arithmetic she does: “she had ninety minutes and that the dolly held six, which meant forty trips, which meant a trip every two minutes and fifteen seconds, including the stairs.” (RL.6.1, explicit evidence)","Cites “the chairs are nobody's job and Wren is eleven and had not learned yet to say that,” and “Mrs. Adeyemi found her at the cage with the dolly tipped on its side,” which shows she was found rather than fetching anyone, and her one word answer, “Nobody.” (RL.6.1, inference with evidence)","Cites “At six o'clock she had done eleven trips and the gym looked exactly as empty as it had at five” against “the whole thing took nineteen minutes.” Alone she had moved 66 chairs in an hour. With eight people the remaining chairs went up in nineteen minutes. (RL.6.1, evidence for a comparison)","She means that being responsible for a job and being left to do it by yourself are not the same thing, and that nobody put the second one on Wren. Cites “You were on the list for chairs... You were not on the list for doing it alone,” and “Who else is doing this,” which is the question she asked first. (RL.6.1, inference with evidence)"]}]},{id:2,title:"Tickets, Miles, Percent",short:"Rates",parts:[{std:["6.RP.A.3"],dur:180,dir:"Use the table for numbers 5 and 6. Work on your paper and hold it up. Say the unit in every answer.",desc:"solving ratio and rate problems, including unit rate, percent, and a ratio table",html:()=>lead("The Neighbor Fund supper sold 240 tickets at $20 each and raised $4,800. The van the trustees are looking at does 20 miles to the gallon, and a round trip to the Harrow clinic is 80 miles."),fig:()=>F.row([milesTable(),"How far the van goes on a gallon"]),qs:["Last year the fund made 40 grants, and 14 of them paid for travel. What percent of the grants were travel grants?","35% of the 240 supper tickets were bought at the door. How many tickets is that?","The trustees want to hold 25% of the $4,800 back as a reserve. How much money is that?","What is the unit rate for a supper ticket, in dollars per ticket? If the supper sold 300 tickets at the same price, how much would it raise?","Use the table. How many gallons does one 80 mile round trip use? At $3.75 a gallon, what does the fuel for one round trip cost?","A $60 grant is what percent of the largest grant the fund has ever made, which was $400?"],key:["35%. 14 out of 40 is 14/40, and 14 ÷ 40 = 0.35, which is 35%. Accept the equivalent ratio 14/40 = 35/100. Or: 40 goes into 100 two and a half times, so 14 becomes 35. Partial: 14/40 written with no percent is approaching. (6.RP.A.3c, percent)","84 tickets. 35% of 240 is 0.35 × 240 = 84. Or: 10% is 24, so 30% is 72 and 5% is 12. Partial: 72 is the 30% only and is approaching. (6.RP.A.3c, finding a percent of a quantity)","$1,200. 25% of 4,800 is 0.25 × 4,800 = 1,200. Also 4,800 ÷ 4. (6.RP.A.3c)","$20 per ticket, because 4,800 ÷ 240 = 20. At that unit rate, 300 tickets raise 300 × 20 = $6,000. Partial: the unit rate right with the 300-ticket total wrong is approaching. (6.RP.A.3b, unit rate)","4 gallons, because the table pairs 80 miles with 4 gallons, and 80 ÷ 20 = 4. The fuel costs 4 × $3.75 = $15.00 for one round trip. Partial: one of the two answers right is approaching. (6.RP.A.3a and 6.RP.A.3b, ratio table and unit rate)","15%. 60 out of 400 is 60/400 = 0.15, which is 15%. Or: 400 goes into 100 four times over, so 60 ÷ 4 = 15. (6.RP.A.3c, percent)"]}]},{id:3,title:"The Neighbor Fund",short:"The Fund",parts:[{std:["RI.6.1"],dur:150,dir:"Read the report to yourself. Then answer out loud. Cite the exact words that support each answer.",desc:"citing textual evidence to support what an informational text says explicitly and inferences drawn from it",html:reportBlock,qs:["Where does the fund's money come from, and how much is there this year? Cite the words.","Work out the average grant over the last five years from the numbers in the report, and cite the sentence you used.","Cite textual evidence to support this inference: the fund cannot save money toward a bigger purchase later.","Cite the evidence that supports this: a grant is meant to settle something once rather than to keep paying for it.","The report never says the trustees expect travel requests to go on rising. Cite the sentence that most strongly supports that inference, and explain how it supports it."],key:["One supper. Cites “Last October it sold 240 tickets at $20 each and raised $4,800, which is the whole of this year's fund.” (RI.6.1, explicit evidence)","$60. Cites “Over the last five years the fund has made 150 grants, totaling $9,000,” and 9,000 ÷ 150 = 60. (RI.6.1, explicit evidence plus arithmetic from the text)","Cites “Last October it sold 240 tickets at $20 each and raised $4,800, which is the whole of this year's fund.” The whole of the year's fund is one supper's takings, so there is no carried balance to build on. Accept a student who also uses “The fund keeps no staff and no office,” as long as the first sentence does the work. (RI.6.1, inference with evidence)","Cites “A grant is for a practical thing that a household cannot cover this month and that will not come back next month.” The phrase “will not come back next month” is the test the trustees apply. (RI.6.1, inference with evidence)","Cites “The trustees report that travel requests have risen every year since the Harrow clinic took over services that used to be at the Westmill surgery.” It supports the inference because the cause named is a permanent move of services, not a one-year event, so the trustees have given a reason for the rise that has not gone away. (RI.6.1, inference with the strongest evidence)"]}]},{id:4,title:"Powers on the Back of a Card",short:"Exponents",parts:[{std:["6.EE.A.1"],dur:130,dir:"Work each one on your paper and hold it up. Say what the exponent is telling you to do.",desc:"writing and evaluating numerical expressions involving whole-number exponents",html:()=>lead("The trustees sketch their sums on the back of a rule card. An exponent says how many times to use the base as a factor."),qs:["Write five cubed as an expression with an exponent, then evaluate it.","Evaluate 2<sup>4</sup> + 3<sup>2</sup>","Write 7 × 7 × 7 × 7 using an exponent, then evaluate it.","Evaluate 10<sup>3</sup> &minus; 4<sup>3</sup>","The gym is set out as a square of tables, 6 along each side. Write the number of tables as an expression with an exponent, and evaluate it.","Which is greater, 3<sup>4</sup> or 4<sup>3</sup>? By how much?"],key:["5³, which is 5 × 5 × 5 = 125. Cubed means the base is used three times as a factor. (6.EE.A.1)","25. 2⁴ = 16 and 3² = 9, and 16 + 9 = 25. Watch for a student who writes 2 × 4 = 8. Partial: one of the two powers right is approaching. (6.EE.A.1)","7⁴ = 2,401. Steps: 7 × 7 = 49, 49 × 7 = 343, 343 × 7 = 2,401. Or: group the factors, 49 × 49 = 2,401. Partial: the power written right with the value wrong is approaching. (6.EE.A.1)","936. 10³ = 1,000 and 4³ = 64, and 1,000 − 64 = 936. Partial: one of the two cubes right is approaching. (6.EE.A.1)","6², which is 6 × 6 = 36 tables. (6.EE.A.1)","3⁴ is greater, by 17. 3⁴ = 81 and 4³ = 64, and 81 − 64 = 17. Swapping the base and the exponent does not give the same value. Partial: the right choice with no difference given is approaching. (6.EE.A.1)"]}]},{id:5,title:"It Had Grown Teeth",short:"Word Choice",parts:[{std:["RL.6.4"],dur:145,dir:"Read the story again. Then answer out loud. Read me the words you are talking about.",desc:"the meaning of words and phrases as used in a story, including figurative and connotative meanings and the effect of word choice on tone",html:storyBlock,qs:["“By the third Friday in October it had grown teeth.” What does that phrase mean here, and what does grown teeth suggest that got bigger would not?","“The kitchen runs on her the way a clock runs on a spring.” What kind of comparison is that, and what does it tell you about Mrs. Adeyemi?","“Then the doors opened and kept opening, and the kitchen came out.” The kitchen is a room. What does the author mean by it, and why write it that way instead of naming seven people?","The chairs go up the stairs “hand to hand, like a bucket brigade.” A bucket brigade is a line of people passing buckets to put out a fire. What does the comparison add?","“It was not a question with a question mark in it.” What does that sentence tell you about how Mrs. Adeyemi said it?","The dolly has “one wheel that fights you.” Why fights instead of sticks? What does that word do to the tone of the paragraph?"],key:["It means the job turned difficult and started to hurt. Grown teeth suggests something alive that can bite back, so the job is not merely larger, it is now working against her. Got bigger would be a measurement. Grown teeth is a threat. (RL.6.4, figurative meaning)","A simile, signaled by “the way.” It says she is not simply one of the kitchen workers but the thing that drives the whole kitchen, the way a spring drives a clock: take her out and it stops. (RL.6.4, figurative language)","He means the people of the kitchen, named by the place they work. Writing it that way makes them arrive as one body rather than as individuals, which matches how fast and how wordlessly they come, and it echoes the line before about Mrs. Adeyemi saying “something short.” Naming seven people would slow the sentence down. (RL.6.4, word choice and its effect)","A bucket brigade is urgent, organized, and made of ordinary neighbors who line up without being told how. The comparison turns the chairs into something closer to an emergency answered in common, and it explains why nineteen minutes was possible. (RL.6.4, figurative language)","That she said it flat, as a statement, not an inquiry. She is not asking for information, she is telling Wren that the answer matters and that she already suspects it. (RL.6.4, connotation and tone)","Fights gives the wheel a will of its own, so even the equipment is against Wren. Sticks would be a fault in a tool. Fights makes the paragraph feel like a struggle against the whole room, which prepares for “it had grown teeth” two paragraphs later. (RL.6.4, the effect of a specific word choice on tone)"]}]},{id:6,title:"Put the Number In",short:"Evaluate",parts:[{std:["6.EE.A.2c"],dur:140,dir:"Work on your paper and hold it up. Say which step you did first in every answer.",desc:"evaluating expressions and formulas at given values, using the order of operations and exponents",html:()=>lead("Each rule below is a formula. Put the given value in for the letter, then work it out in the right order."),qs:["Evaluate 4n + 15 when n = 12","The supper raises t dollars from k tickets at $20 each, so t = 20k. Find t when k = 240.","Evaluate 3(x + 4)<sup>2</sup> when x = 2","A storage cube has edge s. Its volume is V = s<sup>3</sup>. Find V when s = 5 feet, and say the unit.","Evaluate 2a<sup>2</sup> + b when a = 3 and b = 7","The surface area of a cube is A = 6s<sup>2</sup>. Find A when s = 4 inches, and say the unit."],key:["63. 4 × 12 = 48, then 48 + 15 = 63. Multiply before adding. Partial: 108 comes from adding before multiplying and does not count. (6.EE.A.2c)","$4,800. t = 20 × 240 = 4,800. That matches the amount the report says the supper raised. (6.EE.A.2c, evaluating a formula)","108. Parentheses first: 2 + 4 = 6. Then the exponent: 6² = 36. Then 3 × 36 = 108. Watch for 3 × 6 = 18 squared. Partial: 324 comes from squaring 3 times 6 and does not count. (6.EE.A.2c)","125 cubic feet. V = 5³ = 5 × 5 × 5 = 125, and a volume takes cubic units. (6.EE.A.2c, a formula with an exponent)","25. The exponent first: 3² = 9. Then 2 × 9 = 18. Then 18 + 7 = 25. 2a² means 2 × (a × a), not (2 × 3)². Partial: 43 comes from squaring 2a and does not count. (6.EE.A.2c)","96 square inches. 4² = 16, then 6 × 16 = 96, and an area takes square units. Or: count six faces of 16 square inches each. Partial: 96 with no unit, or the wrong unit, is approaching. (6.EE.A.2c, a formula with an exponent)"]}]},{id:7,title:"Buy the Van",short:"The Column",parts:[{std:["RI.6.6"],dur:150,dir:"Read the column to yourself. Then answer out loud. Read me the words you are talking about.",desc:"determining an author's point of view and purpose and explaining how it is conveyed in the text",html:colBlock,qs:["What is Dominic Rhee's purpose in writing this? What tells you it is an opinion column and not a news report?","What is his point of view about small grants? Which words carry it?","Rhee uses the same number the news desk uses, 14 of last year's 40 grants. How does his way of presenting it show his point of view?","In the fourth paragraph he admits that $900 is not $1,450. Why would a writer put his own weakest point in his own column? What does it do for his purpose?","“A grant is a patch. A van is a road.” How do those two sentences convey his point of view?"],key:["His purpose is to persuade the town, and the trustees, to spend the $4,800 on the van before the April meeting. It is an opinion column because it is written in the first person, it is signed, it says outright what the reader should do, and it uses judgments such as “I am done with sixty dollars” rather than only reporting. The source line also labels it an opinion column. (RI.6.6, author's purpose)","He thinks small grants are not working on this problem: they are spending the same money on the same forty miles over and over. The words that carry it are “I am done with sixty dollars,” “one taxi at a time,” and “A grant is a patch.” (RI.6.6, point of view)","He repeats it as a one-word sentence, “Fourteen,” recasts it as “more than a third of everything we did,” and attaches it to “the same forty-mile problem, one taxi at a time.” The news desk gives the number in a plain sentence with the mileage and the reason. Rhee gives the same number a weight and a complaint. (RI.6.6, how point of view is conveyed)","Because answering the objection before a reader raises it makes him sound honest and makes the objection smaller. It also lets him turn the gap into a task with a price on it, $550, which is easier to argue for than a vague shortfall. It serves his purpose, which is persuasion, not disclosure for its own sake. (RI.6.6, how an author conveys and defends a position)","They set two metaphors against each other. A patch is temporary, cheap, and applied to damage that will return. A road is permanent and is the thing itself rather than a repair. Putting them in two short flat sentences makes the choice look obvious, which is what a persuasive ending is for. (RI.6.6, how point of view is conveyed through word choice)"]}]},{id:8,title:"Trips and Dollars",short:"Two Variables",parts:[{std:["6.EE.C.9"],dur:180,dir:"Use Table R and the graph. Work on your paper and hold it up.",desc:"using two variables to represent a changing relationship, writing the equation, and relating it to a table and a graph",html:()=>lead("Fuel for one Harrow round trip costs $15. Let r stand for the number of round trips and c stand for the dollars of fuel spent."),fig:()=>`<div class="figure" style="flex-direction:column;align-items:center;gap:8px">${rideTable()}${rideGraph()}</div>`,qs:["Write an equation for c in terms of r.","Which variable is the independent variable and which is the dependent variable? How do you know?","Use Table R to check your equation at r = 4. Then find c when r = 10, which is not in the table.","Find c when r = 5 two ways: from the graph, and from your equation. Do they agree?","The point (6, 90) is on the graph. Say in words what that point means, and show where the same pair appears in the table.","Suppose the fund had $900 left and spent it on fuel only, at $15 a round trip. Write and solve an equation for the number of round trips that would pay for."],key:["c = 15r. Each round trip adds $15. Or: c = r × 15 or 15r = c count equally. Partial: r = 15c is the reversal and does not count. (6.EE.C.9, writing an equation for two quantities)","r is the independent variable and c is the dependent variable. The number of trips is chosen first and the cost follows from it, not the other way round. Partial: naming both variables with no reason is approaching. (6.EE.C.9)","At r = 4 the equation gives c = 15 × 4 = 60, and Table R shows $60, so they agree. At r = 10, c = 15 × 10 = $150. Partial: one of the two values right is approaching. (6.EE.C.9, relating the equation to the table)","$75 both ways. On the graph, going up from 5 on the horizontal axis meets the line halfway between the $60 and $90 gridlines. From the equation, c = 15 × 5 = 75. They agree. Partial: the right value from one method only is approaching. (6.EE.C.9, relating the equation to the graph)","It means that 6 round trips cost $90 in fuel. The same pair is the last column of Table R, where r = 6 sits above $90. (6.EE.C.9, reading a point on a graph)","60 round trips. The equation is 900 = 15r, so r = 900 ÷ 15 = 60. Check: 60 × 15 = 900. This is only about fuel. The $1,450 insurance and upkeep quote is a separate cost and is not in this equation. Or: count up in 15s, or divide 900 by 15 without writing the equation first. Partial: 60 found with no equation written is approaching. (6.EE.C.9, writing and solving an equation from a situation)"]}]},{id:9,title:"Clause and Case",short:"Grammar",parts:[{title:"One Mistake Each",std:["L.6.1"],dur:90,dir:"Each line has one mistake. Say the whole line out loud the correct way, then name what was wrong with it.",desc:"pronoun case, shifts in person, vague pronouns, and variations from standard English",qs:["Mrs. Adeyemi handed the dolly to Wren and I.","Wren and me carried the last stack up the stairs.","Wren done it on her own for a whole hour.","A volunteer should bring your own apron.","Wren told Mrs. Adeyemi that she had been at the cage since five."],key:["Mrs. Adeyemi handed the dolly to Wren and me. The pronoun follows the preposition to, so it takes the objective case. Test it by dropping the other name: “handed the dolly to me.” (L.6.1a and L.6.1b, pronoun case)","Wren and I carried the last stack up the stairs. The pronoun is part of the subject, so it takes the subjective case. (L.6.1a, pronoun case)","Wren did it on her own for a whole hour. did is the past tense. done needs a helping verb, as in had done. (L.6.1e, recognizing variations from standard English)","A volunteer should bring his or her own apron. Or make it plural: Volunteers should bring their own aprons. The sentence starts in the third person and must stay there. (L.6.1c, correcting a shift in person)","The pronoun she is vague, because it could be Wren or Mrs. Adeyemi. Any clear fix, such as: Wren told Mrs. Adeyemi that she herself had been at the cage since five, or Wren said to Mrs. Adeyemi, “I have been at the cage since five.” (L.6.1d, correcting a vague pronoun)"]},{title:"Say It Right",std:["L.6.1"],dur:70,dir:"Answer out loud. Say whole sentences.",desc:"intensive pronouns, who and whom, pronouns after prepositions, and agreement",qs:["Name the intensive pronoun in this sentence and say what job it is doing: “Mrs. Adeyemi runs the kitchen herself.”","Which is correct, and why? A: Who did the trustees ask? B: Whom did the trustees ask?","Fix this: Between you and I, the van is the better buy.","Fix this: Each of the trustees have a key to the cage."],key:["herself. It is an intensive pronoun. It adds emphasis to Mrs. Adeyemi and could be taken out without breaking the sentence. That is what tells it apart from a reflexive pronoun, which would be needed by the verb. (L.6.1b, intensive pronouns)","B. The pronoun is the object of ask, so the objective form whom is correct. Turn the question into a statement to test it: the trustees asked whom. (L.6.1a, pronoun case)","Between you and me, the van is the better buy. between is a preposition, so the pronoun takes the objective case. (L.6.1a, pronoun case)","Each of the trustees has a key to the cage. The subject is each, which is singular. The phrase of the trustees does not change it. (L.6.1e, standard English usage)"]}]},{id:10,title:"Listen to the Trustees",short:"Listen",parts:[{std:["SL.6.2"],n:5,dur:150,dir:"Listen while your teacher reads the announcement. Then answer out loud. For the last three, use the graph as well.",desc:"interpreting information presented in a text read aloud and in a graph, and explaining how each contributes to the topic",read:ANNOUNCE_READ,html:()=>lead("Your teacher will read you the trustees' announcement from the March town meeting. Listen for what the trustees have decided and what they have not. Then look at the graph."),fig:()=>F.row([grantsGraph(),"Travel grants made each year"]),qs:["Summarize the announcement in three or four sentences.","What are the three things the trustees want the town to know before April?","What does the graph show? Give two details read straight off it.","Rhee's column predicts sixteen travel grants next year. Does the graph support that prediction? Explain what the graph can and cannot settle.","The trustees say they have “the number of those grants and not the shape of them.” Put the announcement and the graph together and explain what they mean."],key:["A summary of the whole, for example: the trustees hold $4,800 and will decide about the van in April, not at this meeting. They want the town to know that the fund cannot hold money over, that the van quote of $3,900 is good until the end of May and includes a six-month engine warranty, and that the $1,450 insurance quote covers four named drivers and would rise with a fifth. They are asking anyone who has used a travel grant to write to them. (SL.6.2, interpreting a text read aloud)","First, the fund cannot carry money from one year to the next, and anything not granted by September goes toward the next supper. Second, the $3,900 van quote is good until the end of May and includes a six-month engine warranty. Third, the $1,450 insurance and upkeep quote is for four named volunteer drivers, and a fifth driver would raise it. (SL.6.2, details from a text read aloud)","It shows travel grants rising every year from 2021 to 2025. Two details read off it: 3 in 2021 and 14 in 2025, or 11 in 2024, or a rise of 3 between 2024 and 2025. (SL.6.2, interpreting information in a graph)","It supports the direction but it cannot settle the number. Every year is higher than the one before, and the yearly rises are 3, 2, 3, and 3, so another rise of 2 or 3, which would put next year at 16 or 17, is a reasonable guess. The graph is five points and nothing more: it cannot tell you whether the rise will continue, flatten, or jump, and it holds no information about next year at all. (SL.6.2, explaining how a graph contributes to a topic and what it does not show)","They mean they can count travel grants but they do not know what each trip was like: who could not go, how long it took, whether a taxi was the only option, whether a shared van would even suit the appointment times. The graph is exactly the number they already have, five bars and nothing behind them, which is why they are asking people to write. (SL.6.2, integrating a read-aloud text with a graph)"]}]},{id:11,title:"Source Desk",short:"Sources",parts:[{std:["W.6.8"],n:4,label:"checks met",dur:150,dir:"Read both sources. Answer numbers 1 and 2 out loud. Do numbers 3 and 4 on your paper and hold it up.",desc:"assessing the credibility of sources, choosing search terms, quoting or paraphrasing without plagiarism, and giving basic bibliographic information",html:()=>SOURCES()+`<ol class="qlist"><li>Which source would you trust for the April decision? Give two reasons drawn from the source information itself, not from whether you agree with it.</li><li>You want one more source. Which search terms are best, and why? A: vans. B: shared vehicle program rural clinic missed appointments. C: how to buy a used minivan.</li><li>Spot the plagiarism. A student's report says, with no quotation marks and no source named: <em>Access to a shared vehicle closed about half of that gap.</em> Fix it on your paper. Quote it correctly, or paraphrase it and name the author.</li><li>Write the basic bibliographic information for Source A: author, title, publisher, and year.</li></ol>`,key:["Source A. Two reasons, such as: it names an author with relevant expertise, it comes from a state health office rather than an anonymous account, it carries a date and is recent, it reports a measured result across five counties, and it gives a specific figure. Source B is an undated comment by an unknown user, is secondhand (“my cousin says”), and offers no evidence for the claim about six days a week. Full credit for two reasons taken from the source lines. (W.6.8, assessing credibility)","B. It uses the key words of the research question, so the results will be about shared vehicles, rural clinics, and missed appointments. A is far too broad and would return anything about vans. C is about buying a vehicle, not about whether one helps. (W.6.8, search terms)","Either fix earns the check. Quote: Dr. Ola Fadel reports that “access to a shared vehicle closed about half of that gap.” Paraphrase: According to Dr. Ola Fadel, a shared vehicle made up roughly half of the difference in missed appointments. The source has to be named either way, and a quotation has to carry quotation marks. (W.6.8, quoting or paraphrasing without plagiarism)","Has all four parts. Example: Fadel, Ola. “Rural Clinic Consolidation and Patient Travel: A Five-County Study.” State Rural Health Office, 2025. Any clear, consistent order is fine as long as author, title, publisher, and year are all there. (W.6.8, basic bibliographic information)"]}]},{id:12,title:"Brief the Trustees",short:"Brief",parts:[{std:["SL.6.4"],n:5,label:"checks met",dur:140,dir:"Plan for 1 minute. Then talk for 1 to 2 minutes. Look at the camera and speak clearly.",desc:"a 1 to 2 minute spoken brief that presents a claim and findings in a logical order, with facts and details",html:()=>`<p class="big">The trustees hold $4,800 and will decide in April. Tell them what to do with it. Make your claim, give your two strongest findings from the table, name the drawback of your own choice, and say what you need from them.</p>${choiceTable()}${`<div class="row center" style="margin-top:16px;gap:14px;flex-wrap:wrap">${["My claim","Finding 1","Finding 2","The drawback","What I need"].map(x=>`<span class="pill" style="font-size:1.1rem">${x}</span>`).join("")}</div>`}`,key:["States a clear claim up front and keeps to it. Either option can be argued. The van case rests on 14 of 40 grants going to the same trip and on the five-year rise from 3 to 14. The grants case rests on about 80 one-off needs of any kind against one fixed use, and on the $550 gap. (SL.6.4, presenting a claim)","Sequences the ideas logically rather than reading the table across, and keeps to the most important points (SL.6.4)","Uses pertinent facts and details from the table accurately, with the numbers quoted correctly, such as $3,900 and $900, $4,800 and about 80 grants, $1,450, $550, or the series 3, 6, 8, 11, 14 (SL.6.4)","Names the drawback of the student's own choice. For the van: the fund is $550 short on insurance and upkeep in the first year, and the van only serves one kind of need. For the grants: 14 of 40 grants went to the same problem last year, and the fund has never held money over, so nothing accumulates. (SL.6.4, pertinent descriptions and details)","Appropriate eye contact with the camera, adequate volume, and clear pronunciation for 1 to 2 minutes (SL.6.4)"]}]},{id:13,title:"The Story and the Poem",short:"Two Forms",parts:[{std:["RL.6.9"],n:3,dur:55,dir:"Read the poem. Then answer out loud about the poem and the story together.",desc:"comparing a story and a poem in how each one handles the same theme",html:()=>poemBlock()+`<details class="look-back" open style="margin-top:10px"><summary>Look back at the story</summary>${storyBlock()}</details>`,qs:["What theme do the story and the poem share?","The poem and the story are different forms. How does each one get that theme across?","Which text states the idea most plainly, and what does the other one do instead?"],key:["That the work does not get lighter, but it stops being one person's. Mrs. Adeyemi puts it as two different lists, being on the list for chairs and not on the list for doing it alone. The poem puts it as more of us beneath it. Any wording of that shared idea counts","The story uses characters, events over ninety minutes, and dialogue, so the reader watches it happen. The poem has no characters and no plot: it repeats a line, and it works by images, the room as empty as a drum and the stairs as a river running up","The story states it plainly in the last two lines of dialogue. The poem never states it: it leaves the reader with an image and lets the idea sit underneath it"]}]},{id:14,title:"Two Writers, One Fund",short:"Two Writers",parts:[{std:["RI.6.9"],n:3,dur:55,dir:"Look at the report and the column. Answer out loud.",desc:"comparing how two authors present the same events and figures",html:()=>`<details class="look-back" open><summary>The report</summary>${reportBlock()}</details><details class="look-back" open><summary>The column</summary>${colBlock()}</details>`,qs:["Both authors use the same figure, 14 of last year's 40 grants. How does each one present it?","Both mention the $4,800. How does each one frame that number?","Name one thing one author reports that the other leaves out, and say why that matters."],key:["The news desk states it once, as one fact in a list, and attaches a cause: travel requests have risen since the Harrow clinic took over services. Rhee repeats it, sets it alone as a one word sentence, recasts it as more than a third of everything we did, and projects it forward to 16 and then 18","The report calls it the whole of this year's fund, raised from 240 tickets at $20. Rhee treats it as money already in hand and measures it against a $3,900 price, so the $4,800 becomes a budget rather than a result","Any real one, with a reason. The report gives the five-year record, 150 grants totaling $9,000, and the largest grant of $400, which Rhee never mentions and which makes the fund look steady rather than stuck. Rhee gives the van price and the $1,450 insurance quote, which the report never gives, so a reader of the report alone cannot price the idea"]}]},{id:15,title:"Three Short Writes",short:"Writes",parts:[{title:"Make the Case",std:["W.6.1"],n:3,label:"checks met",dur:60,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"a one paragraph argument with a claim and evidence from the texts",html:()=>lead("Should the fund buy the van? Write one paragraph. State your claim, then support it with two pieces of evidence from the report or the column.")+checklist(["Begin with your claim","Give two pieces of evidence, with the numbers right","Say in one sentence why that evidence supports the claim"]),key:["States a clear claim and keeps to it. Either side can be argued (W.6.1a)","Supports it with two relevant pieces of evidence from the texts, quoted accurately, such as 14 of 40 grants, the rise from 3 to 14, $3,900 against $4,800, the $1,450 quote and the $550 gap, or the 150 grants over five years (W.6.1b)","Links the evidence to the claim with a reason, using a word or phrase such as because, since, this shows, or therefore (W.6.1c)","One paragraph is the whole job here. Do not ask for more."]},{title:"Explain It",std:["W.6.2"],n:3,label:"checks met",dur:60,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"a one paragraph explanation of what the fund is and how a grant works",html:()=>lead("Somebody has just moved to Westmill and has never heard of the Neighbor Fund. Write one paragraph that tells them what it is and how a grant works.")+checklist(["Introduce the topic in the first sentence","Give two facts from the report","Use the words the topic needs, such as grant, trustee, supper"]),key:["Introduces the topic so a reader who knows nothing can follow (W.6.2a)","Develops it with two relevant facts from the report, such as one supper on the third Friday in October, 240 tickets at $20 raising $4,800, three trustees holding the checkbook, or the rule that a grant is for a practical thing a household cannot cover this month (W.6.2b)","Uses precise, topic-specific language and does not drift into opinion (W.6.2d)","One paragraph is the whole job here. Do not ask for more."]},{title:"In Her Words",std:["W.6.3"],n:3,label:"checks met",dur:60,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"a one paragraph narrative from a character's point of view",html:()=>lead("Write one paragraph as Wren, about the moment the double doors opened and the kitchen came out. Tell it in order, the way she saw it.")+checklist(["Set up where you are and what you can see","Use one line of dialogue","Use one detail of sound, smell, or sight","End the moment"]),key:["Establishes the point of view and the situation, with Wren as the narrator (W.6.3a)","Uses at least one narrative technique, a line of dialogue or a piece of description, and keeps the events in order (W.6.3b and W.6.3c)","Uses a concrete sensory detail and closes the moment rather than stopping mid sentence (W.6.3d and W.6.3e)","Details have to fit the story: the aprons, the smell of onions, the dolly with the wheel that fights you, the chairs going hand to hand. One paragraph is the whole job here."]}]},{id:16,title:"Dividing in the Kitchen",short:"Divide",parts:[{std:["6.NS.A.1"],n:3,dur:45,dir:"Solve on your paper. Say your answer and what the division means.",desc:"dividing a fraction by a fraction and saying what the quotient means",qs:["Mrs. Adeyemi has 3/4 of a tray of food left, and one serving is 1/8 of a tray. How many servings is that?","Work out 2/3 divided by 1/6.","Say in words what 5/6 divided by 1/3 asks, then work it out."],key:["6 servings. 3/4 divided by 1/8 = 3/4 x 8/1 = 6. Or: common denominators, 6/8 ÷ 1/8 = 6 ÷ 1 = 6.","4. Multiply by the reciprocal: 2/3 x 6/1 = 4. Or: common denominators, 4/6 ÷ 1/6 = 4 ÷ 1 = 4.","It asks how many thirds fit into 5/6. The answer is 2 and 1/2, because 5/6 x 3/1 = 15/6. Partial: the right value with no account of what the division asks is approaching."]}]},{id:17,title:"Above and Below Zero",short:"Signed",parts:[{title:"Opposite Directions",std:["6.NS.C.5"],n:3,dur:45,dir:"Answer out loud.",desc:"using positive and negative numbers for quantities in opposite directions, and reading what zero means",qs:["After buying the van, the fund would hold $900 and owe $1,450 for the first year of cover. Write that shortfall as a signed number, and say what 0 would mean here.","What does the opposite of that number mean for the fund?","If a $20 supper ticket sold is +20, what is a $60 grant paid out? Why do the two get opposite signs?"],key:["Negative 550. Zero would mean the fund had exactly enough to cover the year, with nothing over and nothing owing. Partial: negative 550 with no account of what 0 would mean is approaching.","Positive 550, the amount the fund would have to raise to close the gap","Negative 60. Money coming in and money going out move the balance in opposite directions, so one is positive and the other is negative. Partial: negative 60 with no account of the opposite directions is approaching."]},{title:"Points on the Grid",std:["6.NS.C.6"],n:3,dur:45,dir:"Look at the grid. Answer out loud.",desc:"placing rational numbers on a number line and reading points with negative coordinates",fig:()=>F.row(F.coordGrid({max:5,quad4:true,size:360,points:[[-3,2,"P"],[3,-2,"Q"]]})),qs:["What are the coordinates of P and of Q?","Both of Q's signs are the opposite of P's. What does that do to where the point sits?","On a number line, where does negative 2.5 sit, and what is the opposite of negative 3?"],key:["P is (-3, 2) and Q is (3, -2). The order matters: across first, then up or down. Partial: one of the two points right is approaching.","It reflects the point across both axes, so Q is the same distance from the origin in the opposite direction. P is left and up, Q is right and down. Or: calling it a half turn about the origin counts as the same thing. Partial: naming one axis only is approaching.","Negative 2.5 sits exactly halfway between negative 2 and negative 3, to the left of zero. The opposite of negative 3 is positive 3, the same distance from zero on the other side. Partial: one of the two questions right is approaching."]}]},{id:18,title:"Plots and Crates",short:"Area Volume",parts:[{title:"Two Plots",std:["6.G.A.1"],n:3,dur:50,dir:"Look at the two plots. Solve on your paper and say your answers.",desc:"finding the area of a triangle and of a trapezoid by formula and by decomposing",fig:()=>F.row(areaFig()),qs:["What is the area of Plot 1?","Cut Plot 2 along the two dashed lines. What three pieces do you get, and what is the area of each?","What is the total area of Plot 2?"],key:["30 square metres. Half of 10 x 6. Or: 1/2 × 10 × 6 straight from the formula. Partial: 60 square metres is the whole rectangle and does not count.","A rectangle 6 m by 4 m, which is 24 square metres, and a triangle at each end with a base of 2 m and a height of 4 m, which is 4 square metres each. Partial: the three pieces named with one area wrong is approaching.","32 square metres. 24 + 4 + 4, which is the same as the trapezoid rule, half of 10 plus 6, times 4. Partial: the rectangle alone, 24, is approaching."]},{title:"Fractional Crates",std:["6.G.A.2"],n:3,dur:45,dir:"Solve on your paper. Say your answer with its unit.",desc:"volume of a right rectangular prism with fractional edge lengths",qs:["A crate measures 1/2 m by 3/4 m by 2/3 m. What is its volume?","A second crate is 2 and 1/2 ft by 2 ft by 1 and 1/2 ft. What is its volume?","How many cubes with an edge of 1/2 unit fit inside a cube with an edge of 1 unit, and what is each little cube worth?"],key:["1/4 of a cubic metre. (1/2)(3/4)(2/3) = 6/24. Or: cancel first, since the 3s cancel and 1/2 × 2/4 = 1/4.","7 and 1/2 cubic feet. 2.5 x 2 x 1.5. Or: 5/2 × 2 × 3/2 = 30/4 = 7 1/2 in fractions.","8 of them, and each one is worth 1/8 of a cubic unit. 8 x 1/8 = 1, which is why the formula still works with fractional edges. Partial: 8 cubes right with no value for one cube is approaching."]}]},{id:19,title:"Five Years of Travel Grants",short:"Data",parts:[{std:["6.SP.B.5"],n:3,dur:50,dir:"Look at the graph. Solve on your paper and say your answers.",desc:"summarizing a numerical data set: how many observations, what was measured, a measure of centre, and the spread",fig:()=>F.row(grantsGraph()),qs:["How many observations are in this data set, and what was measured each time?","Work out the mean and the median.","Describe the spread, and say which measure of centre describes this set better and why."],key:["5 observations, one for each year from 2021 to 2025. What was measured is the number of travel grants the fund made in that year. Partial: the count right with nothing said about what was measured is approaching.","The values are 3, 6, 8, 11, 14. The mean is 8.4, because 42 divided by 5 is 8.4. The median is 8, the middle value when they are in order. Partial: one of the mean and the median right is approaching.","The range is 11, from 3 up to 14, and the values rise every year with no value out on its own. Either measure of centre is defensible here, but the set is not level: it is a trend, so a single centre hides the rise. Give the check for any answer that names the rise as the important feature. Partial: the range given with nothing said about the rise is approaching."]}]},{id:20,title:"The Pump and the Pipes",short:"Systems",parts:[{std:["LS1"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Answer out loud.",desc:"explaining how body systems interact to supply cells with what they need",html:()=>`<p class="big">The heart is an organ made of muscle tissue. It pushes blood through vessels to every part of the body and back again.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("heart",74)}${ico("drop",74)}${ico("run",74)}</div>`,qs:["Name three things blood carries to a muscle cell or away from it, and say which direction each one goes.","The heart, the lungs and the digestive system are parts of three different systems. Explain how all three are needed to keep one muscle cell working.","Put these in order from smallest to largest, then give the heart’s place in the list: cell, organ, tissue, organ system."],key:["To the cell: oxygen and nutrients such as sugar. Away from the cell: carbon dioxide and other wastes. Thermal energy is also carried around. Partial: three things named with the direction given for only two of them is approaching. (LS1)","The digestive system breaks food into molecules the body can absorb, the lungs take in oxygen and release carbon dioxide, and the circulatory system carries both the nutrients and the oxygen to the cell and takes the waste away. If any one stops, the cell cannot work. Partial: two of the three systems explained is approaching, and the digestive system is usually the one left out of the chain. (LS1)","Cell, tissue, organ, organ system. The heart is an organ, made of muscle tissue built from cells, and it is part of the circulatory system. Partial: the four in the right order with the heart not placed as an organ within the circulatory system is approaching. (LS1)"]},{std:["LS1"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"interpreting data on the body's response to activity",html:()=>`<p class="big">A class recorded one person’s pulse and breathing.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>When</th><th>Beats a minute</th><th>Breaths a minute</th></tr>
     <tr><td>sitting still</td><td>72</td><td>14</td></tr>
     <tr><td>after walking fast</td><td>104</td><td>22</td></tr>
     <tr><td>after running</td><td>148</td><td>36</td></tr>
     <tr><td>five minutes later</td><td>84</td><td>16</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("heart",74)}${ico("run",74)}${ico("clock",74)}</div>`,qs:["Both numbers rise together as the activity gets harder. Explain why the body does that.","Five minutes after running, the numbers are near the sitting values again. What does returning to normal show about the body?","A person says the heart beats faster so the blood can cool down. Using the data and what you know, say whether that explanation is right and why."],key:["Working muscles need more oxygen and more sugar and produce more carbon dioxide, so the lungs take in air faster and the heart pumps faster to move blood through more quickly. The two systems respond together. Partial: saying the muscles need more oxygen, with nothing said about carbon dioxide having to be carried away as well, is approaching. (LS1)","That the body senses conditions and adjusts to bring them back to a steady state, raising the rates when demand is high and lowering them when demand falls. Partial: saying the body goes back to normal when the effort stops, with nothing said about it sensing conditions and adjusting to hold a steady state, is approaching. (LS1)","No. The main reason is supplying oxygen and nutrients and removing carbon dioxide from working muscles. Blood does carry thermal energy toward the skin, which is part of cooling, but it is not why the rate rises with effort. Partial: answering no, with nothing said about the real reason being supply of oxygen and removal of carbon dioxide, is approaching. (LS1)"]}]},{id:21,title:"Family Resemblance",short:"Heredity",parts:[{std:["LS3"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"sorting inherited from acquired traits and explaining the role of genes",html:()=>`<p class="big">A class listed things about themselves and tried to sort them.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Where it came from, according to the class</th></tr>
     <tr><td>eye colour</td><td>"both my parents have it"</td></tr>
     <tr><td>a scar on one knee</td><td>"I fell off my bike"</td></tr>
     <tr><td>speaking two languages</td><td>"we speak both at home"</td></tr>
     <tr><td>attached earlobes</td><td>"my mother has them too"</td></tr>
     <tr><td>a strong throwing arm</td><td>"I practise every day"</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("face",74)}${ico("soccerBall",74)}${ico("books",74)}</div>`,qs:["Sort these five into inherited and not inherited, and give the test you used.","A strong throwing arm comes from practice, and yet some people build strength faster than others. Explain how both inheritance and experience can affect one feature.","Where in the body are inherited instructions kept, and how does a person come to have two versions of each one?"],key:["Inherited: eye colour and attached earlobes. Not inherited: the scar, speaking two languages, and the throwing arm. The test is whether it comes from the genes a person was born with rather than from something that happened during their life. Partial: all five sorted correctly with no test stated for how the sorting was done is approaching. (LS3)","Genes can affect things like muscle type and build, which set how a body responds, while practice is what actually builds the strength. The result comes from both, so a feature can be partly influenced by genes without being inherited as such. Partial: saying it is a bit of both, with nothing said about genes setting how the body responds while the practice builds the strength, is approaching. (LS3)","In genes on chromosomes in the nucleus of each cell. A person receives one set of chromosomes from each parent, so there are two versions of each gene. Partial: naming genes or the nucleus with nothing said about one set of chromosomes coming from each parent is approaching. (LS3)"]},{std:["LS3"],n:3,dur:80,dir:"Answer out loud.",desc:"explaining variation among siblings and the effect of a change in the genes",html:()=>`<p class="big">Two children in one family look quite different from each other, and both resemble their parents in some ways. A third family has identical twins who share all the same genes.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("face",74)}${ico("hands",74)}${ico("heart",74)}</div>`,qs:["Two children of the same parents are not alike. Explain why, using what each child receives.","Identical twins have the same genes and still differ in some ways, such as fitness or a scar. Explain how that is possible.","A change in a gene can be harmful, harmless or helpful. Give one example of each and say what makes the difference."],key:["Each child receives a different combination of gene versions, one set from each parent, and which versions are passed on is a matter of chance, so siblings share some traits and differ in others. Partial: saying each child gets a different mix, with nothing said about which versions are passed on being a matter of chance, is approaching. (LS3)","Genes are not the only influence. Experience, diet, activity, illness and accidents all act on the body during life, and those differences do not come from the genes. Partial: naming the accident that caused the scar, with nothing said about diet, activity or illness acting on the body during life, is approaching. (LS3)","Harmful: a change that stops a needed protein being made. Harmless: a change in a part of the instructions that alters nothing the body does. Helpful: a change that works better in the conditions the organism lives in. What makes the difference is the effect on how well the organism functions where it lives. Partial: two of the three cases given is approaching, and the harmless case is usually the one left out. (LS3)"]}]},{id:22,title:"Reading the Sky",short:"Weather",parts:[{std:["ESS2"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Look at the map description. Answer out loud.",desc:"interpreting weather information to explain air masses and precipitation",html:()=>`<p class="big">A weather report describes a cold air mass pushing in from the north west. It pushes against warm moist air sitting over the region. There is rain along the line where they meet, and clearing behind it.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("cloud",74)}${ico("rain",74)}${ico("wind",74)}</div>`,qs:["Why does rain form along the line where the two air masses meet?","Warm air is described as moist and cold air as dry. Explain why warm air can hold more water vapour.","After the line passes, the sky clears and the temperature drops. Explain both changes."],key:["The cold air is denser, so it pushes under the warm air and lifts it. Rising air cools, its water vapour condenses into cloud droplets, and when they grow heavy enough they fall as rain. Partial: saying the warm air is lifted, with nothing said about the rising air cooling so its vapour condenses, is approaching. (ESS2)","The warmer the air, the more water vapour it can hold before the vapour condenses, so warm air masses typically carry more moisture and cold ones carry less. Partial: saying warm air is wetter, without the point that warmer air can hold more vapour before it condenses, is approaching. (ESS2)","The warm moist air has been pushed away and the cold dry air is now overhead. There is little moisture to form cloud, so it clears, and the air itself is colder, so the temperature falls. Partial: accounting for the temperature drop with nothing said about there being too little moisture left to form cloud, or the other way round, is approaching. (ESS2)"]},{std:["ESS2"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Answer out loud.",desc:"explaining the water cycle and how water moves through a community's surroundings",html:()=>`<p class="big">Rain falls on a neighbourhood with roofs, roads, lawns and a park with trees and a pond.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("drop",74)}${ico("tree",74)}${ico("wave",74)}</div>`,qs:["Name three different paths a raindrop can take after it lands here, and where each one leads.","The pond loses water on a hot day even without anyone taking any out. Explain where it goes and what drives it.","Trees in the park move water from the soil into the air. Name that process and explain why it matters to the water cycle."],key:["Any three with a destination: run off a roof or road into a drain and on to a stream, soak into a lawn and become groundwater, be taken up by roots and released from leaves, evaporate from a surface, or collect in the pond. Partial: two paths named with destinations where three were asked for is approaching. (ESS2)","It evaporates into the air as water vapour. Energy from the sun gives water molecules enough energy to leave the surface, and warm dry moving air carries them away. Partial: naming evaporation with nothing said about energy from the sun driving it is approaching. (ESS2)","Transpiration. It returns large amounts of water from soil to the atmosphere, where it can form cloud and fall again, so plants are a major part of how water moves between ground and air. Partial: naming transpiration with nothing said about why it matters to the cycle is approaching, and describing the process without the word is approaching too. (ESS2)"]}]},{id:23,title:"A Garden for the Block",short:"Impact",parts:[{std:["ESS3"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"evaluating a community change for its effects on local water, soil and heat",html:()=>`<p class="big">A vacant paved lot was turned into a community garden. Measurements were taken before and one year after.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th></th><th>Paved lot</th><th>After the garden</th></tr>
     <tr><td>Surface temperature at noon in summer</td><td>49 °C</td><td>31 °C</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Rain running into the storm drain</td><td>nearly all</td><td>about a third</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Insect species counted</td><td>4</td><td>23</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("sprout",74)}${ico("sun",74)}${ico("bee",74)}</div>`,qs:["Explain why the surface is 18 degrees cooler after the change.","Less rain reaches the storm drain now. Where is the rest of it going, and why is that better for the creek the drain empties into?","Insect species went from 4 to 23. Name one way that change helps the garden itself."],key:["Plants and soil absorb less and reflect more than dark pavement, and plants release water vapour, which carries energy away as it evaporates. Shade also keeps sunlight off the ground. Partial: naming the shade alone, with nothing said about soil and plants absorbing less than dark pavement, is approaching. (ESS3)","It soaks into the soil and is taken up by plants or becomes groundwater. That is better for the creek because less water arrives at once, so the creek rises less sharply and carries less heat and pollution off the pavement. Partial: saying the water soaks in, with nothing said about why less water arriving at once is better for the creek, is approaching. (ESS3)","More pollinators, so more fruit and seed, and more predator insects that eat pests, so less damage to the crops. A more varied set of species is also more stable. Partial: saying more insects is good for nature, with no benefit named to the garden itself, is approaching. (ESS3)"]},{std:["ESS3"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Answer out loud.",desc:"weighing resource use and designing a way to test a claim about a community choice",html:()=>`<p class="big">The garden is watered from a tap. A neighbour proposes catching rain off the shed roof in barrels instead, and says it would cover most of the garden’s needs.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("wateringCan",74)}${ico("drop",74)}${ico("box",74)}</div>`,qs:["What two things would you have to measure to know whether the roof could supply most of the garden’s water?","Caught rainwater and tap water both come from precipitation in the end. Why is catching rain on site still a saving?","Name one limitation of relying only on caught rain, and one thing the garden could do about it."],key:["How much water the garden uses over a season, and how much rain falls on the shed roof over that same season, which depends on rainfall and roof area. Partial: one of the two quantities named where two were asked for is approaching. (ESS3)","Tap water has been collected, stored, treated and pumped, which uses energy and infrastructure, and it draws on a supply shared with everyone else. Rain caught on site needs none of that. Partial: saying rain is free, with nothing said about the energy and treatment behind tap water, is approaching. (ESS3)","Limitation: rain does not fall when the garden needs it most, which is usually the dry season. The garden could add more storage to carry water from the wet season, or keep the tap as a backup. Partial: naming the limitation with no measure offered to deal with it is approaching. (ESS3)"]}]},{id:24,title:"Energy in a Body",short:"Energy",parts:[{std:["PS3"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Answer out loud.",desc:"tracing energy transfers in a moving body and relating kinetic energy to mass and speed",html:()=>`<p class="big">A student runs up a flight of stairs, then walks back down.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("run",74)}${ico("mountain",74)}${ico("heart",74)}</div>`,qs:["Running up the stairs stores energy. What kind, and where did the energy to store it come from?","Two students of different mass run up the same stairs. Which stores more energy at the top, and why?","Why is a person warm and breathing hard after running up the stairs, in terms of energy?"],key:["Gravitational potential energy, stored because the body is higher than it was. It came from food energy released in the student’s cells. Partial: naming the stored energy with nothing said about it coming from food energy released in the cells is approaching. (PS3)","The one with more mass, because gravitational potential energy depends on mass as well as on the height climbed, and the height is the same for both. Partial: choosing the heavier student with nothing said about the height climbed being the same for both is approaching. (PS3)","Food energy released in the cells is not all turned into movement. A large share leaves as thermal energy, which warms the body, and the fast breathing is supplying the oxygen needed to keep releasing that energy. Partial: saying the body gets hot from working, with nothing said about a large share of the released energy leaving as thermal energy, is approaching. (PS3)"]},{std:["PS3"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"interpreting data on thermal energy transfer between objects",html:()=>`<p class="big">A mug of hot cocoa was poured for someone coming in from outside, and temperatures were recorded.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Minutes</th><th>Cocoa</th><th>Mug handle</th><th>Room air</th></tr>
     <tr><td>0</td><td>78 °C</td><td>21 °C</td><td>21 °C</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>70 °C</td><td>38 °C</td><td>21 °C</td></tr>
     <tr><td>10</td><td>52 °C</td><td>34 °C</td><td>21 °C</td></tr>
     <tr><td>40</td><td>23 °C</td><td>22 °C</td><td>21 °C</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("cocoa",74)}${ico("thermometer",74)}</div>`,qs:["Describe the direction energy moved during these forty minutes, and name what it moved between.","At forty minutes everything is near 21 degrees. What is that condition called, and will the cocoa cool below the room?","Someone wraps the mug in a scarf at minute two. Predict what the ten minute reading would have been and explain."],key:["From the hot cocoa into the mug, then into the room air and the table. Energy always moves from the warmer object to the cooler one until they are at the same temperature. Partial: naming the direction with nothing said about what the energy moved between is approaching. (PS3)","Thermal equilibrium. No. Once it matches the room there is no temperature difference to drive a transfer, so it stays at room temperature. Partial: answering no correctly without naming thermal equilibrium is approaching. (PS3)","Higher than 52 degrees, because a scarf is a poor conductor and traps air, so energy would leave the cocoa more slowly. It would still cool, just at a lower rate. Partial: predicting a higher reading with nothing said about the scarf conducting poorly and trapping air is approaching, and predicting that it would not cool at all is not yet there. (PS3)"]}]},{id:25,title:"Designing to Help",short:"Design",parts:[{std:["ETS1"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Answer out loud. Write your criteria and constraints on your paper.",desc:"defining a design problem for a real user with criteria and constraints",html:()=>`<p class="big">A neighbour who cannot easily bend down has trouble picking up the newspaper from the path. Available: a broom handle, wire, strong tape, a rubber band, a bulldog clip, and one afternoon. The tool has to be used with one hand and must weigh less than a full water bottle.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("hand",74)}${ico("books",74)}${ico("ruler",74)}</div>`,qs:["State the problem in one sentence that names the need, not the device.","Write two criteria that could be measured and two constraints from the description.","Why does asking the neighbour to try the tool matter more than deciding yourself whether it works?"],key:["Something like: pick up a newspaper from the path without bending down. Partial: a sentence that names a grabber or a stick rather than the result to be achieved is approaching. (ETS1)","Criteria: picks up the paper in a set number of tries, holds it without dropping, can be used one handed. Constraints: only the listed materials, one afternoon, one handed use, weight under that of a full water bottle. Partial: two criteria and one constraint, or one of each, is approaching. (ETS1)","The neighbour is the person the tool has to work for, so their grip, reach and comfort are the real test. A designer can meet every number and still build something the user cannot hold. Partial: saying the neighbour should try it to see if she likes it, with nothing said about her grip and reach being the real test, is approaching. (ETS1)"]},{std:["ETS1"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Look at the test results. Answer out loud.",desc:"comparing tested designs against criteria and planning a combined next version",html:()=>`<p class="big">Three tools were tried, ten pickups each, by the person who will use it.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Tool</th><th>Successful pickups</th><th>One handed?</th><th>Weight</th></tr>
     <tr><td>A: clip taped to the handle</td><td>9 of 10</td><td>yes</td><td>light</td></tr>
     <tr><td>B: wire hook on the handle</td><td>5 of 10</td><td>yes</td><td>very light</td></tr>
     <tr><td>C: clip plus a wire basket</td><td>10 of 10</td><td>no, needs two hands</td><td>heavy</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("hand",74)}${ico("box",74)}${ico("clock",74)}</div>`,qs:["Which tool should be chosen, and what in the data supports it?","Tool C worked every time and still fails. Explain which requirements it breaks.","Name the strongest feature of each tool, then describe the next version to test and what result would show the change worked."],key:["Tool A. Nine of ten pickups, one handed, and light, so it meets both constraints while coming close to the best result. Partial: naming Tool A with no number from the data used to support it is approaching. (ETS1)","It needs two hands and it is heavy, so it breaks the one handed constraint and the weight constraint. Working perfectly on the criterion does not rescue a design that breaks the limits. Partial: naming one of the two constraints Tool C breaks is approaching. (ETS1)","A: reliable clip, light and one handed. B: lightest. C: the basket that catches the paper every time. Next version: tool A with a small light wire guide instead of the full basket, tested with another ten pickups, with success being ten of ten while staying one handed and light. Partial: a strongest feature given for each tool with no next version and no result named is approaching. (ETS1)"]}]},{id:26,title:"Evidence of Care",short:"Care",parts:[{std:["HSS-6.1"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Read the dig notes. Answer out loud.",desc:"reading remains and burials as evidence of care and of differences between people",html:()=>`<p class="big">Three notes from a dig at an early village:</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Note</th><th>What the diggers wrote</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>One person’s leg bone had been broken and had healed, set straight.</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>An older person had lost most teeth years before death, and the grinding stones nearby show grain worked to a very fine flour.</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>Of twenty burials, three held beads, a polished stone bowl and a copper pin. The rest held nothing.</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("hands",74)}${ico("heart",74)}${ico("gem",74)}</div>`,qs:["A broken leg that healed straight tells you something about the group this person lived in. What is it?","Someone with almost no teeth lived for years, and the village ground grain into a very fine flour. What connection can you draw, and how certain is it?","Three burials out of twenty held valuable objects. What does that pattern suggest about the society, and what does it not tell you?"],key:["Someone cared for the injured person: a person with a broken leg cannot gather food, carry or keep up, so they were fed and sheltered for weeks while it healed, and someone set it. Care of the helpless leaves physical evidence. Partial: naming the healed break with nothing said about being fed and sheltered is approaching. (HSS-6.1)","The fine flour could be food this person could eat without teeth, which would mean the village adjusted to keep an elder alive. It is a reasonable reading, not a proven one, since fine flour has other uses. Credit the student for naming the uncertainty. Partial: stating it as proved rather than a reasonable reading is approaching. (HSS-6.1)","That people were not all equal: some had wealth or standing marked at burial. It does not tell you why those three were different, whether rank was inherited, or what the objects meant to them. Partial: naming the inequality with nothing said about what the burials cannot tell you is approaching. (HSS-6.1)"]},{std:["HSS-6.2"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing what Mesopotamian law and the Egyptian idea of balance required of people",html:()=>`<p class="big">Hammurabi’s laws set penalties by the rank of the person harmed. So the same act cost more if done to a wealthier person. The same collection also protected widows and orphans, set limits on debts and fixed what a worker must be paid. In Egypt, officials and ordinary people alike were expected to act according to ma’at, a word that covered order, balance and fair dealing, and an official who took more than his share was said to have broken it.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("board",74)}${ico("hands",74)}${ico("sun",74)}</div>`,qs:["Hammurabi’s laws protect widows and orphans and also set lighter penalties for harming a poorer person. What do those two features together tell you about Mesopotamian society?","A written set of wages and debt limits is an economic law as much as a moral one. What problem was it trying to solve?","Egypt expected officials to act according to ma’at, a standard of order and fair dealing. How does holding officials to a shared idea like that work differently from a written penalty?"],key:["It was a ranked society in which people were not equal before the law, and at the same time it treated protecting the weakest as the ruler’s duty. Both things were true at once. Partial: one of the two things named is approaching, since both were true at once. (HSS-6.2)","People with nothing being squeezed: unpayable debts, unpaid work and disputes over pay. Fixing wages and limiting debt keeps workers from falling into permanent dependence and keeps disputes out of the street. Partial: naming the problem with nothing said about what the rules prevent is approaching. (HSS-6.2)","A penalty punishes after the fact and only what is written down. A shared standard shapes what an official thinks is expected of him across situations no law listed, and the judgment is made by the community rather than a court. It is weaker to enforce and wider in reach. Partial: one of the two kinds of standard described is approaching. (HSS-6.2)"]}]},{id:27,title:"What the Law Required",short:"Required",parts:[{std:["HSS-6.3"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing Hebrew law as a code of obligations toward other people",html:()=>`<p class="big">Judaism is the religion of the Ancient Hebrews. It drew its law and its ethical teaching from the Torah. That law set out obligations toward other people, not only toward God. It required fair weights in trade. It required wages paid the same day to a hired worker. It required the return of a lost animal to its owner. It required consideration for the widow, the orphan and the stranger. Ten short commands, remembered as the Ten Commandments, included rules against stealing, lying in court and taking what belongs to a neighbor.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("hands",74)}${ico("ruler",74)}${ico("sheep",74)}</div>`,qs:["A law requiring a hired worker to be paid the same day targets one specific hardship. What is it?","Several of the Torah's rules are about honesty in trade and in court rather than about belief. Why would a religious law code spend so much of itself on everyday dealings?","The law names the widow, the orphan and the stranger again and again. What does repeating those three groups tell you about who was most at risk in that society?"],key:["A day laborer with no savings has nothing to eat that night if payment is held back, so delay alone can ruin someone who did the work. Partial: saying it is unfair with no account of having nothing to eat that night is approaching. (HSS-6.3)","Because in that belief how a person treats other people is part of the obligation itself, so honest weights and truthful testimony are religious duties, not separate from them. It also makes the law usable in daily disputes. Partial: treating honest dealing and religious duty as separate is the misreading to watch for and is approaching. (HSS-6.3)","That people without a man’s land or household standing had no protection by custom: no inheritance, no family to speak for them, no local standing. Law had to supply what family and property otherwise provided. Partial: naming the groups with nothing said about what law had to supply is approaching. (HSS-6.3)"]},{std:["HSS-6.4"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing Greek guest obligation, citizen juries and the physician's standard",html:()=>`<p class="big">Greeks held to a strong custom of guest obligation: a traveler who arrived at a door was to be fed and sheltered before being asked his business, and a host who broke that custom was shamed. Athenian courts were juries of hundreds of ordinary citizens, chosen by lot, who heard both sides and voted. Greek physicians were held to a standard that began with the obligation to do no harm to the patient.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("hands",74)}${ico("bread",74)}${ico("herb",74)}</div>`,qs:["Why would a society with little law outside its own city-state need a strong custom about treating travelers well?","An Athenian jury could hold hundreds of citizens chosen by lot. Name one advantage and one weakness of deciding a case that way.","A physician’s standard that starts with doing no harm is an obligation to the patient. What does it tell you about Greek thinking that such a standard was written down at all?"],key:["Because outside your own city nothing protected you: no government, no police, no shared court. A custom that obliges strangers to shelter strangers is the only thing making travel and trade possible. Partial: calling it good manners with no account of there being no protection is approaching. (HSS-6.4)","Advantage: hard to bribe or intimidate hundreds of people, and the verdict is the citizens’ own rather than an official’s. Weakness: no training in law, and a large crowd can be swayed by a skilled speaker rather than by evidence. Partial: one of the advantage and the weakness given is approaching. (HSS-6.4)","That they were working out general rules of conduct by reasoning about a role rather than taking rules only from custom or from the gods, which is also what Greek philosophy was doing. Partial: describing the oath with nothing said about reasoning out general rules is approaching. (HSS-6.4)"]}]},{id:28,title:"Duty Toward Others",short:"Duty",parts:[{std:["HSS-6.5"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing non-harm, compassion and a ruler's public obligations in early India",html:()=>`<p class="big">Hinduism, Buddhism and Jainism all began in India. They developed the idea of ahimsa, not causing harm to living things. It shaped diet, work and law for many people. Buddhism held that compassion for others followed from understanding that suffering is shared. The emperor Asoka had messages carved in public. They stated that he expected officials to deal gently with people. They stated that he had planted shade trees, dug wells and built rest houses along the roads.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("leaf",74)}${ico("tree",74)}${ico("drop",74)}</div>`,qs:["Ahimsa, not causing harm to living things, affects more than how a person feels. Name two parts of ordinary life it would change.","Asoka listed wells, shade trees and rest houses in his public messages. Why would a ruler want those works recorded in stone?","A belief held by many people can change what a government does. Using the wells and the rest houses as your example, explain how."],key:["Any two with a reason: what people ate, since meat is ruled out for many; what work they could do, since butchering, hunting and some farming conflict with it; how animals were treated; and how law and punishment were argued about. Partial: one area named with a reason is approaching, since the question asks for two. (HSS-6.5)","They are proof of care for travelers and subjects, which supports his authority, and permanent public credit outlasts anything said aloud. It also tells officials across the empire what standard he expects. Partial: naming the public credit with nothing said about telling officials the standard is approaching. (HSS-6.5)","When many people hold that harm is wrong and that care for others matters, a ruler gains standing by doing visible good and loses it by being harsh, so the belief becomes a political reason to spend money on public works. Partial: saying the ruler was kind with no account of how belief became a political reason is approaching. (HSS-6.5)"]},{std:["HSS-6.6"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing Confucian humaneness and family duty as the basis of Chinese social order",html:()=>`<p class="big">Confucianism is the teaching of Confucius. It held that the center of good conduct was ren, usually translated as humaneness or fellow feeling. He put it in a short rule. Do not do to others what you would not want done to you. Confucianism also held that duty began at home, with a child’s respect for parents. A ruler who treated the people as a good parent treats a child would be followed willingly.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("books",74)}${ico("heart",74)}${ico("hands",74)}</div>`,qs:["The Confucian rule is stated as a negative: do not do what you would not want done to you. What is the practical difference between that and a rule telling you what you should do?","Why would a teaching that starts duty inside the family be useful to someone trying to hold a large state together?","Confucianism placed duties on the ruler, not only on the people. Name one duty it would place on a ruler, and what the people could say if he failed it."],key:["A negative rule sets a floor by ruling out harm, and anyone can follow it anywhere. A positive rule asks you to decide what good to do, which is more demanding and easier to get wrong on someone else’s behalf. Either direction can be credited if the student explains. Partial: one of the two kinds of rule described is approaching. (HSS-6.6)","It teaches obedience and obligation in the place everyone already is, so the habit of meeting duties is learned young and then extends outward to officials and ruler. Social order is built from the household up rather than imposed only from above. Partial: naming the family with nothing said about the habit extending to ruler and state is approaching. (HSS-6.6)","Duties such as feeding the people, governing by good example, keeping taxes bearable or appointing honest officials. If he failed, people could argue he had forfeited the right to be followed, which is the same reasoning as losing heaven’s approval. Partial: one duty named with nothing said about forfeiting the right to be followed is approaching. (HSS-6.6)"]},{std:["HSS-6.7"],n:3,dur:125,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing Roman patronage, plebeian rights and charitable practice in the empire",html:()=>`<p class="big">Roman society ran on patronage. A wealthy patron gave help, money, legal backing and favors. His clients owed him support, service and public respect in return. Plebeians won the right to their own officials, the tribunes, who could block an action that harmed a plebeian. Christianity began in the Roman province of Judea with the teachings of Jesus of Nazareth. It spread through the empire. Roman law outlawed the practice and persecuted Christians for nearly three centuries, seizing property and putting some to death. The emperor Constantine legalized it in 313. Even while it was unlawful, Christian communities in the empire became known for organized giving. It went to the poor, widows and the sick. Their own members paid for it.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("coin",74)}${ico("hands",74)}${ico("bread",74)}</div>`,qs:["Patronage was help given in exchange for support. Name one way it helped an ordinary Roman and one way it kept him unequal.","The tribunes could block an action against a plebeian. Why is the power to stop something valuable even without the power to pass laws?","Organized giving to the poor, paid for by a group’s own members, was unusual in the empire, and the Christians doing it were breaking Roman law at the time. Why would a practice like that draw people to a group?"],key:["Helped: money in hard times, a powerful voice in court, work and introductions. Kept unequal: the help was a favor he had to repay with loyalty and public service, so he stayed dependent rather than gaining standing of his own. Partial: one of the helped and the kept unequal given is approaching. (HSS-6.7)","A veto protects: it stops harm before it happens and forces the powerful to negotiate rather than simply act. Whoever can stop a measure must be dealt with, which is real political weight. Partial: saying a veto stops a law with nothing said about forcing the powerful to negotiate is approaching. (HSS-6.7)","Because it offered practical security that nothing else in the empire offered to poor people: food, care in illness and burial, plus belonging to a group that would act for you. Credit a student who adds that people joined even though the practice was unlawful, which shows how much that support was worth. Any answer naming the practical support counts. Partial: naming the belief alone, with no practical support named, is approaching. (HSS-6.7)"]}]}];const LEVELUPS={1:{id:"L1",title:"Level Up: Several Pieces",std:["RL.7.1"],needs:["RL.6.1"],n:2,dir:"Answer out loud. Give several separate pieces of evidence for each one.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Cite several pieces of evidence to support this analysis: the story treats being left alone with a job as the real problem, not the size of the job.</li><li>Cite several pieces of evidence for what Mrs. Adeyemi is like. Use at least three, from different parts of the story.</li></ol>${storyBlock()}`,key:["Several pieces, for example: the opening reason the chairs are Wren's at all, “the chairs are nobody's job”; the first thing Mrs. Adeyemi says is not about chairs but about people, “Who else is doing this”; the job that defeated Wren for an hour took nineteen minutes with eight people, so the work was never beyond the town, only beyond one person; and the last line separates the two things outright, “You were on the list for chairs... You were not on the list for doing it alone.” (RL.7.1, several pieces of evidence)","At least three, for example: she is seventy and still runs the kitchen; “the kitchen runs on her the way a clock runs on a spring”; she asks one question and does not wait to be asked for help; she says “something short” and seven people come; she is “already back at the pots” before Wren can thank her; and she corrects Wren's thanks into a lesson rather than accepting it. (RL.7.1, several pieces of evidence)"]},2:{id:"L2",title:"Level Up: Percent Up and Down",std:["7.RP.A.3"],needs:["6.RP.A.3"],n:3,dir:"Work on your paper and hold it up. Show the percent you used.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>The supper ticket goes from $20 to $23. What is the percent increase?</li><li>A dealer takes 12% off the $3,900 van. What is the new price?</li><li>The trustees want next year's supper to raise 15% more than this year's $4,800. How much would that be?</li></ol>`,key:["15%. The increase is 23 − 20 = 3, and 3 ÷ 20 = 0.15, which is 15%. The percent is always taken on the original amount. (7.RP.A.3, percent increase)","$3,432. Take 12% off means pay 88%: 0.88 × 3,900 = 3,432. Check the other way: 0.12 × 3,900 = 468, and 3,900 − 468 = 3,432. (7.RP.A.3, percent decrease)","$5,520. A 15% increase means 115% of the original: 1.15 × 4,800 = 5,520. Check: 0.15 × 4,800 = 720, and 4,800 + 720 = 5,520. (7.RP.A.3, percent increase)"]},6:{id:"L6",title:"Level Up: Move the Letters",std:["7.EE.A.1"],needs:["6.EE.A.2c"],n:3,dir:"Work on your paper and hold it up.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Expand 4(2x + 5)</li><li>Factor 18r + 24</li><li>Simplify 15r &minus; 6 + 5r + 14</li></ol>`,key:["8x + 20. Multiply both terms inside the parentheses by 4. (7.EE.A.1, expanding a linear expression)","6(3r + 4). The greatest common factor of 18 and 24 is 6, and 18 ÷ 6 = 3 and 24 ÷ 6 = 4. Check by expanding: 6 × 3r = 18r and 6 × 4 = 24. (7.EE.A.1, factoring)","20r + 8. Collect the r terms: 15r + 5r = 20r. Collect the numbers: −6 + 14 = 8. (7.EE.A.1, adding like terms)"]},8:{id:"L8",title:"Level Up: Is It Proportional?",std:["7.RP.A.2"],needs:["6.EE.C.9"],n:3,dir:"Answer out loud and say how you decided.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Is c = 15r a proportional relationship? Give two tests it passes.</li><li>The fund starts the year with $4,800 and spends $60 a grant, so the money left is m = 4800 &minus; 60g. Is that proportional? Explain.</li><li>For the relationship that is proportional, name the constant of proportionality and say what it means here. Which point on the graph shows it?</li></ol>${rideTable()}`,key:["Yes. Test one: every pair in Table R gives the same quotient, 30 ÷ 2 = 15, 60 ÷ 4 = 15, 90 ÷ 6 = 15. Test two: the graph is a straight line through the origin, (0, 0), because 0 trips cost $0. (7.RP.A.2a)","No. At g = 0 the money left is $4,800, so the graph does not pass through the origin, and the quotients are not constant: 4,800 ÷ 0 is not even defined, and 4,740 ÷ 1 is not the same as 4,680 ÷ 2. It changes at a constant rate, which is not the same thing as being proportional. (7.RP.A.2a)","15, the dollars of fuel for one round trip. On the graph it is the point (1, 15), which is where the line crosses the gridline above 1 on the horizontal axis. (7.RP.A.2b and 7.RP.A.2d)"]},9:{id:"L9",title:"Level Up: Phrase or Clause",std:["L.7.1"],needs:["L.6.1"],n:3,dir:"Say each answer out loud, then name the phrase or clause you used.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Name the function of the underlined part: “Wren worked out, <u>before she had moved a single chair</u>, that she needed forty trips.” Is it a phrase or a clause, and what job does it do?</li><li>Name the type of this sentence: “Mrs. Adeyemi went back through the double doors, and the kitchen came out.”</li><li>Fix the misplaced modifier: “Tipped on its side, Wren found the dolly at the cage.”</li></ol>`,key:["A clause, because it has a subject, she, and a verb, had moved. It works like an adverb, telling when. (L.7.1a, phrases and clauses)","Compound. Two independent clauses joined by the comma and the conjunction and. (L.7.1b, sentence types)","Wren found the dolly tipped on its side at the cage. As written, the phrase sits next to Wren and says she was tipped over. Move it beside the dolly. (L.7.1c, misplaced modifiers)"]},7:{id:"L7",title:"Level Up: Two Writers, One Fund",std:["RI.7.6"],needs:["RI.6.6"],n:2,dir:"Answer out loud. Use details from both texts.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Both texts give the fact that 14 of last year's 40 grants paid for travel to Harrow. How does each writer distinguish his or her position from other positions on that fact?</li><li>What is the news desk's point of view in the report? Is a report without an opinion still written from a position? Use details.</li></ol><div class="texts"><article>${srcLine(REPORT_SRC)}${para(REPORT,"margin:0 0 .55em")}</article><article>${srcLine(COL_SRC)}${para(COL,"margin:0 0 .55em")}</article></div>`,key:["The report sets the number beside its cause and leaves the judgment open: it gives the 40 miles, says Harrow is “now the nearest place for several kinds of appointment,” and attributes the rise to the trustees rather than asserting it. Rhee separates himself from everyone still writing checks: “I am done with sixty dollars,” “one taxi at a time,” and the patch and road metaphor at the end. He also anticipates the opposing position, the $1,450, and answers it in his own voice. (RI.7.6, how an author distinguishes a position from others)","The report's position is that the fund is a set of facts a reader should be able to check: it gives sources for its claims, keeps the trustees' report as the trustees' report, and ends on an arithmetic point rather than a recommendation. That is still a position, because it decides what counts as the story. It includes the cost structure and the rules card and excludes any account of what a missed appointment costs a household, which is the thing Rhee's column is built on. (RI.7.6, author's point of view in a text that states no opinion)"]}};const READ_STD=["RL.6.1","RL.6.4","RL.6.9","RI.6.1","RI.6.6","RI.6.9","RL.7.1","RI.7.6"];const WRITE_STD=["W.6.1","W.6.2","W.6.3","W.6.8"];const MATH_STD=["6.RP.A.3","6.EE.A.1","6.EE.A.2c","6.EE.C.9","6.NS.A.1","6.NS.C.5","6.NS.C.6","6.G.A.1","6.G.A.2","6.SP.B.5","7.RP.A.3","7.EE.A.1","7.RP.A.2"];const G_FUND=[["trustee","a person trusted to hold and spend money for other people"],["grant","money given for a stated purpose and not paid back"],["reserve","money set aside and not spent this year"],["quote","a price an seller promises to hold for a stated time"],["upkeep","the cost of keeping something working"],["warranty","a promise to repair a fault free for a stated time"],["consolidation","joining separate services together in one place"]];const G_STORY=[["dolly","a small low platform on wheels for moving heavy things"],["cage","a locked wire store room"],["bucket brigade","a line of people passing buckets hand to hand to put out a fire"],["apron","a cloth worn over clothes while cooking"],["spring","the coiled metal part that drives an old clock"]];const G_SRC=[["credible","able to be trusted"],["plagiarism","using someone else's words or ideas as if they were your own"],["paraphrase","to say it in your own words"],["bibliographic information","author, title, publisher, and year"],["secondhand","heard from someone else rather than seen"],["economist","someone who studies how money and resources are used"]];const G_TERMS=[["point of view","the way one writer sees the subject, and what the writer wants the reader to think"],["purpose","the reason the writer wrote it"],["connotation","the feeling a word carries beyond its plain meaning"],["figurative language","words that mean something other than what they literally say"],["simile","a comparison using like or as, or a phrase such as the way"],["metaphor","saying one thing is another thing"],["tone","the attitude the writing takes toward its subject"]];function glossHtml(st){const s=st.std;if(s.includes("W.6.8"))return dl(G_SRC.concat(G_FUND));if(s.includes("RI.6.6")||s.includes("RI.7.6")||s.includes("RL.6.4"))return dl(G_TERMS.concat(G_STORY));if(s.includes("RI.6.1")||s.includes("SL.6.2")||s.includes("SL.6.4"))return dl(G_FUND);return dl(G_STORY)}const FR={"RL.6.1":["I think ____. The words that prove it are “____.”","The story says, “____.” This shows that ____."],"RL.7.1":["One piece of evidence is “____.”","Another is “____.”","A third is “____.” Together they show ____."],"RI.6.1":["The report says, “____.” This tells me ____.","I can tell that ____ because the report says “____.”"],"RL.6.4":["The phrase ____ means ____.","It is a ____ (simile, metaphor), and it makes the reader see ____.","The author chose ____ instead of ____, which makes the tone ____."],"RI.6.6":["Rhee wrote this column to ____.","I can tell it is one person's point of view because ____.","He uses ____ instead of ____, which shows he wants readers to ____."],"RI.7.6":["The report treats ____ as ____.","The column treats the same fact as ____.","Rhee sets himself apart from ____ by ____."],"SL.6.2":["The announcement said ____.","The graph shows ____. The numbers are ____ and ____.","Together they tell me ____, because ____."],"SL.6.4":["The trustees should ____.","One finding is ____.","The table also shows that ____.","The drawback of my choice is ____.","From the trustees I need ____."],"W.6.8":["I trust Source ____ more because ____ and ____.","These search terms are best because ____.","Dr. Ola Fadel reports, “____.”","According to Dr. Ola Fadel, ____.","Author. “Title.” Publisher, year."],"RL.6.9":["Both texts are about ____.","The story shows it by ____.","The poem does it instead by ____."],"RI.6.9":["The report presents ____ as ____.","The column presents the same figure as ____.","One of them leaves out ____, which matters because ____."],"W.6.1":["My claim is ____.","One piece of evidence is ____.","A second is ____.","This supports my claim because ____."],"W.6.2":["The Neighbor Fund is ____.","It works like this: ____.","One fact from the report is ____."],"W.6.3":["I was ____ when ____.","“____,” ____ said.","I could ____ (hear, smell, see) ____.","By the time ____, ____."],"L.6.1":["The correct sentence is ____, because ____.","I would say it this way: ____.","“____” is unclear because it could mean ____ or ____."],"L.7.1":["It is a ____ because ____.","This sentence is ____ because it has ____.","The modifier belongs next to ____."]};function framesHtml(st){const a=[];st.std.forEach(x=>(FR[x]||[]).forEach(f=>{if(!a.includes(f))a.push(f)}));return`<ul class="starters" style="font-size:1.2rem">${a.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`}function gramHtml(){return`<h4>Pronoun case</h4>`+dl([["Subjective (does the action)","I, you, he, she, it, we, they, who. <em>Wren and I carried it.</em>"],["Objective (receives the action, or follows a preposition)","me, you, him, her, it, us, them, whom. <em>She handed it to Wren and me.</em>"],["Possessive","my, your, his, her, its, our, their, whose."],["Quick test","Drop the other name and say it alone: “to me,” not “to I.”"]])+`<h4>Intensive pronouns</h4>`+dl([["Intensive","myself, yourself, himself, herself, itself, ourselves, themselves, used to stress the noun just before it. <em>She runs the kitchen herself.</em> Take it out and the sentence still works."]])+`<h4>Three things to watch</h4>`+dl([["Shift in person","Do not start in the third person and switch to you. Pick one and keep it."],["Vague pronoun","If a pronoun could point at two different people, name the person instead."],["Standard English","did, not done, without a helping verb. Each is singular, so it takes a singular verb."]])+`<h4>Phrases, clauses, and modifiers</h4>`+dl([["Clause","has a subject and a verb. <em>before she had moved a chair</em>"],["Phrase","has no subject and verb pair. <em>on a flat dolly</em>"],["Compound sentence","two independent clauses joined by and, but, or, so."],["Misplaced modifier","the describing phrase sits next to the wrong word. Move it beside the word it describes."]])}const MULT_N=12;let multSel=[0,0];function multHtml(){let h=`<div class="tool-scroll"><table class="mult"><tr><th>×</th>`;for(let c=1;c<=MULT_N;c++)h+=`<th data-r="0" data-c="${c}" class="${multSel[1]===c?"hl":""}">${c}</th>`;h+="</tr>";for(let r=1;r<=MULT_N;r++){h+=`<tr><th data-r="${r}" data-c="0" class="${multSel[0]===r?"hl":""}">${r}</th>`;for(let c=1;c<=MULT_N;c++){const hr=multSel[0]===r,hc=multSel[1]===c;h+=`<td data-r="${r}" data-c="${c}" class="${hr&&hc?"pick":hr||hc?"hl":""}">${r*c}</td>`}h+="</tr>"}return h+`</table></div><p class="small">Click a number to light up its row and column. Click it again to clear.</p>`}const multBind=(b,r)=>b.querySelectorAll("[data-r]").forEach(c=>c.onclick=()=>{const x=+c.dataset.r,k=+c.dataset.c;multSel=multSel[0]===x&&multSel[1]===k?[0,0]:[x,k];r()});const fm=t=>`<p class="formula">${t}</p>`;const FORM={"6.RP.A.3":["Ratios, rates, and percent",ul(["<strong>Unit rate:</strong> divide to get the amount for one. $4,800 for 240 tickets is 4,800 ÷ 240 = $20 each.","<strong>Percent:</strong> a percent is a rate out of 100. 14 out of 40 is 14 ÷ 40 = 0.35 = 35%.","<strong>Percent of a quantity:</strong> change the percent to a decimal and multiply. 35% of 240 is 0.35 × 240.","<strong>Ratio table:</strong> multiply or divide both rows by the same number to get a new pair."])],"6.EE.A.1":["Exponents",ul(["An exponent says how many times to use the base as a factor. 5³ = 5 × 5 × 5.","5³ is not 5 × 3. 2⁴ = 16, not 8.","Squared means the exponent 2. Cubed means the exponent 3.","Swapping the base and the exponent changes the value: 3⁴ = 81 but 4³ = 64."])],"6.EE.A.2c":["Evaluating expressions",ul(["Put the number in for the letter first, then work it out.","Order: grouping symbols, then exponents, then multiply and divide, then add and subtract.","2a² means 2 × (a × a). The exponent only sticks to the a.","An area answer takes square units. A volume answer takes cubic units."])],"6.EE.C.9":["Two variables that change together",ul(["The independent variable is the one you choose. The dependent variable is the one that follows.","Write the equation from the rule, then test it against every column of the table.","On the graph, each column of the table is one point: (independent, dependent).","To answer a question backwards, put the known value in and solve for the letter."])],"7.RP.A.2":["Proportional relationships",fm("y = kx")+ul(["Every pair gives the same quotient y ÷ x, and that quotient is k.","The graph is a straight line through the origin, (0, 0).","The point (1, k) on the graph shows the unit rate.","A constant rate of change is not always proportional. Check (0, 0)."])],"7.RP.A.3":["Percent increase and decrease",ul(["Percent change is always taken on the original amount.","Increase of p%: multiply by (1 + p as a decimal). A 15% rise is × 1.15.","Decrease of p%: multiply by (1 − p as a decimal). 12% off is × 0.88.","Check by finding the change on its own and adding or subtracting it."])],"7.EE.A.1":["Linear expressions",ul(["Expand: multiply everything inside the parentheses by the number outside.","Factor: pull out the greatest common factor of every term.","Like terms have the same letter. 15r + 5r = 20r. 15r + 5 does not combine.","Check a factoring by expanding it again."])]};const formHtml=st=>st.std.filter(x=>FORM[x]).map(x=>`<h4>${FORM[x][0]}</h4>${FORM[x][1]}`).join("");let calcE="",calcAns="";function calcEval(src){const s=src.replace(/×/g,"*").replace(/÷/g,"/").replace(/−/g,"-");let i=0;if(!s)return"";const num=()=>{const a=i;while(i<s.length&&/[0-9.]/.test(s[i]))i++;const v=a===i?NaN:Number(s.slice(a,i));if(!isFinite(v))throw new Error("n");return v};const fac=()=>{if(s[i]==="-"){i++;return-fac()}if(s[i]==="("){i++;const v=expr();if(s[i]===")")i++;return v}return num()};const pow=()=>{let v=fac();if(s[i]==="^"){i++;v=Math.pow(v,pow())}return v};const term=()=>{let v=pow();for(;;){if(s[i]==="*"){i++;v*=pow()}else if(s[i]==="/"){i++;v/=pow()}else return v}};function expr(){let v=term();for(;;){if(s[i]==="+"){i++;v+=term()}else if(s[i]==="-"){i++;v-=term()}else return v}}try{const v=expr();if(i<s.length||!isFinite(v))return"Error";return String(+v.toPrecision(10))}catch(e){return"Error"}}function calcHtml(){const keys=["7","8","9","÷","⌫","4","5","6","×","(","1","2","3","−",")","0",".","^","+","C","="];return`<div class="calc-disp" aria-live="polite">${esc(calcE)||"0"}${calcAns!==""?`<br><strong>= ${esc(calcAns)}</strong>`:""}</div><div class="calc-keys">${keys.map(k=>`<button data-k="${k}" class="${k==="="?"eq":/[0-9.]/.test(k)?"":"op"}">${k==="^"?"xʸ":k}</button>`).join("")}</div><p class="small">Press xʸ for an exponent: 5 xʸ 3 is 5³. Type a percent as a decimal: 35% is 0.35.</p>`}function calcBind(b,r){b.querySelectorAll("[data-k]").forEach(x=>x.onclick=()=>{const k=x.dataset.k;if(k==="C"){calcE="";calcAns=""}else if(k==="⌫"){calcE=calcE.slice(0,-1);calcAns=""}else if(k==="="){calcAns=calcEval(calcE)}else{if(calcAns!==""&&calcAns!=="Error"&&/^[+−×÷^]$/.test(k))calcE=calcAns.replace("-","−");else if(calcAns!=="")calcE="";calcAns="";calcE+=k}r()})}let gridPts=[],gridJoin=false;function pgridHtml(){const lines=[];if(gridJoin)for(let i=1;i<gridPts.length;i++)lines.push([gridPts[i-1],gridPts[i]]);return`<div class="blk-row"><button class="chip" data-g="join" aria-pressed="${gridJoin}">Connect the points</button><button class="chip" data-g="undo">Undo</button><button class="chip" data-g="clear">Clear</button></div><div class="figure" data-pg style="cursor:crosshair">${F.coordGrid({max:12,size:380,points:gridPts,lines})}</div><p class="small">Click the grid to plot a point. Points: ${gridPts.map(p=>`(${p[0]}, ${p[1]})`).join(", ")||"none yet"}</p>`}function pgridBind(b,r){b.querySelectorAll("[data-g]").forEach(x=>x.onclick=()=>{const g=x.dataset.g;if(g==="join")gridJoin=!gridJoin;if(g==="undo")gridPts.pop();if(g==="clear")gridPts=[];r()});const el=b.querySelector("[data-pg] svg");if(!el)return;const S=380,pad=34,u=(S-2*pad)/12;el.onclick=e=>{const q=el.getBoundingClientRect(),vx=(e.clientX-q.left)*S/q.width,vy=(e.clientY-q.top)*S/q.height,x=Math.round((vx-pad)/u),y=Math.round((S-pad-vy)/u);if(x<0||x>12||y<0||y>12)return;gridPts.push([x,y]);r()}}const TOOLS={gloss:{name:"Word help",std:READ_STD.concat(WRITE_STD,["SL.6.2","SL.6.4","L.6.1"]),html:glossHtml},frames:{name:"Sentence frames",std:Object.keys(FR),html:framesHtml},gram:{name:"Grammar card",std:["L.6.1","L.7.1"],html:gramHtml},form:{name:"Formula and definition card",std:Object.keys(FORM),html:formHtml},mult:{name:"Multiplication chart",std:MATH_STD,html:multHtml,bind:multBind},calc:{name:"Calculator",std:MATH_STD,html:calcHtml,bind:calcBind},grid:{name:"Coordinate grid",std:["6.EE.C.9","7.RP.A.2"],html:pgridHtml,bind:pgridBind}};const SUP_CAVEATS=[{t:"passage",std:READ_STD,msg:"If the text was read to the student, this stop shows listening comprehension, not reading."},{t:"frames",std:["W.6.1","W.6.2","W.6.3"],msg:"Sentence frames supply the opening, the order, and the linking words, so count those checks as supported, not independent."},{t:"form",std:["6.NS.A.1","6.G.A.1","6.G.A.2","6.SP.B.5"],msg:"The card gives the reciprocal rule, the area formulas, the volume formula, and how to find a mean, so count those answers as supported, not independent."},{t:"calc",std:["6.NS.A.1","6.G.A.1","6.G.A.2","6.SP.B.5"],msg:"The calculator did the arithmetic. Rate whether the student chose the right operation and can say what the answer means."},{t:"grid",std:["6.NS.C.6"],msg:"The grid tool shows the four quadrants and the signs, so reading a point off it is supported. The reflection question is still the student's own."},{t:"passage",std:["W.6.1","W.6.2","W.6.3"],msg:"If the report, the column, or the story was read to the student, the writing checks still stand, but note that the evidence was supplied by ear."},{t:"passage",std:["W.6.8","SL.6.2","SL.6.4"],msg:"If you read the source cards, the table, or the graph aloud, note it. The checks on this stop still stand."},{t:"gloss",msg:"Word help explains background words such as trustee, quote, and bucket brigade. It does not give answers, but note that it was used."},{t:"gloss",std:["RL.6.4"],msg:"This stop is about working out what words mean in the text. The glossary defines simile, metaphor, connotation, and tone but not the phrases being asked about, so it does not lower the rating. If you explained grown teeth, bucket brigade, or fights, mark Word or phrase explained by teacher instead and count that question as supported."},{t:"frames",std:READ_STD,msg:"Sentence frames give the shape of the answer. Rate the evidence and the thinking the student supplied."},{t:"frames",std:["W.6.8","SL.6.2","SL.6.4"],msg:"Sentence frames supply the order and the linking words, so count those checks as supported, not independent."},{t:"gram",std:["L.6.1","L.7.1"],msg:"The grammar card names the three pronoun cases, the intensive pronouns, the errors to watch for, and the phrase and clause definitions, so this stop shows using a reference rather than recall."},{t:"form",std:["6.RP.A.3","7.RP.A.3"],msg:"The card gives the method for unit rate, for percent of a quantity, and for percent change, so count those answers as supported, not independent."},{t:"form",std:["6.EE.A.1","6.EE.A.2c","7.EE.A.1"],msg:"The card states what an exponent means, the order of operations, and the rules for expanding and factoring, so this stop then shows applying stated rules rather than recalling them."},{t:"form",std:["6.EE.C.9","7.RP.A.2"],msg:"The card names the independent and dependent variables and gives the test for a proportional relationship, including the check at the origin, so count the naming and sorting questions as supported."},{t:"mult",std:MATH_STD,msg:"The multiplication chart supplied the facts. Rate the setup and the reasoning."},{t:"calc",std:["6.RP.A.3","7.RP.A.3"],msg:"The calculator did the percent arithmetic. Rate whether the student knew what to divide by and what the result means."},{t:"calc",std:["6.EE.A.1","6.EE.A.2c","7.EE.A.1"],msg:"The calculator has an exponent key, so rate whether the student knew what the exponent meant and which step came first, not the computation."},{t:"calc",std:["6.EE.C.9","7.RP.A.2"],msg:"The calculator worked out the values. Rate whether the student wrote the right equation and could read it against the table and the graph."},{t:"grid",std:["6.EE.C.9","7.RP.A.2"],msg:"If the student plotted the pairs on the grid tool rather than reading the printed graph, note it. The student should still explain what the graph shows."},{t:"hint",msg:"Note which question got the hint, and count that question as supported."},{t:"fewer",msg:"If questions were skipped, change the “of” number in the score to the number the student tried."}];return{stops:STOPS,levelups:LEVELUPS,tools:TOOLS,supCaveats:SUP_CAVEATS,passageStd:READ_STD.concat(["W.6.8"])}}});
